/* Wau-Bun — Part 1, the same scenes retold in a modern
   horror-suspense voice: the events, people, places and turns of the 1856
   text, in present-day English with the dread it actually carried. Original
   prose written for this app — nothing is quoted from any other book.
   Generated — do not hand-edit; the passages live in wau-bun/dark/<scene>.txt.
   Loaded only when a reader opens this mode. */
var WAUBUN_DARK_PART1 = {
  s1: ["We went aboard the steamer Henry Clay on a dark, rainy evening in September of 1830, bound for Green Bay. Everyone we knew in Detroit had told us how fortunate we were. The alternative was passage in one of the little schooners that were, at that time, the ordinary means of reaching the few scattered settlements on Lakes Huron and Michigan, and nobody who had made that crossing could speak of it without a certain change coming over the face.", "They all had a story ready, their own or a friend's. Days held fast on the St. Clair flats with nothing to do but wait out the water. Head-winds off Thunder Bay that came on as though something had aimed them. Calms at Mackinac, calms at the Manitous, a flat lake and a slack sail and no way of knowing how long it would last. The account that made us feel luckiest of all was true, and it belonged to our own family: a relative had left Detroit in the month of June and did not set foot in Chicago until the September following. Three months. A sail-vessel has been known to do the same run in four days.", "None of it would have deterred me. I was going to see that country at last, and not merely see it — live in it. For years it had been a region of romance to me. When I was a child my greatest pleasure had been the letters of a dear relative describing his home and his mode of life in the Indian country; later there was his happy account of the tour he made with General Cass, in 1820, to the sources of the Mississippi. Earlier than either, there was a long word I had spelled out at my teacher's knee, Mich-i-li-mack-i-nac, syllable by syllable. Vast lakes. Boundless prairies. Forests with no end to them. Now I was to see it. It was to be my home.", "We laughed the whole way to the quay. The streets of Detroit were unpaved and deep in mud, and a cart was the only vehicle that could get through them, so a cart carried us along the dark by-ways down to the water. We laughed again going below — the stair to the little room they called the Ladies' Cabin was narrow and near enough to upright to be called a ladder. The accommodations delighted us. Measured against a schooner they were the very height of comfort and convenience, and better still, the cabin held nobody but myself, one other lady, and two little girls.", "For the first twenty-four hours nothing could have been pleasanter. Some of the passengers were officers, old friends of ours. We had brought plenty of books, and now and then one of the gentlemen read aloud from them, and broke off to admire the country going past — the untouched woods, the enormous reach of water with not one sail anywhere upon it, all of it magnificent and all of it empty — and then turned to a cigar, or to a game of euchre, to get through the hours."],
  s2: ["For a while the passage over Thunder Bay was delightful. It was not destined, in our favor, to belie its name. A storm came on fast and furious, and worse than its fury was its duration. The pitching and rolling of the little boat, the closeness below, even the sea-sickness — we bore all of it as became us. They were what we had expected and had prepared for. Then a new feature of our discomfort appeared, and it almost upset our philosophy.", "The rain fell in torrents, and it found its way through every seam and pore of deck and moulding. It came down the stairway. It came through the joints and the crevices. It saturated the carpet first, then the bedding, until at last we were driven, by stress of weather, into the Gentlemen's Cabin. Way was made for us very gallantly there. Every provision was resorted to for our comfort, and we were congratulating ourselves on having found a haven in our distress when the seams above us opened, and such a flood came down upon our devoted heads that even umbrellas were no protection at all. Nothing was left for the ladies and the children but the berths, which in that apartment had fortunately stayed dry, and there we stayed ensconced the whole day long. Our dinner was served up to us on our pillows. The gentlemen chose the driest spots they could find, raised their umbrellas, and sat under them telling amusing anecdotes and saying funny things to cheer us, hour after hour, until the rain ceased at last and at nine o'clock in the evening we were gladdened by the news that we had reached the pier at Mackinac."],
  s3: ["Mr. and Mrs. Robert Stuart received us with the most affectionate cordiality. We had been expected at their hospitable house for some days.", "The repose and the comfort of an asylum like that one can only be truly appreciated by people who have reached it after a tossing and a drenching such as ours had been. There was a bright, warm fire. There were faces beaming with the kindest interest. Between the two of them, every sensation of fatigue and annoyance was gone.", "After a season of pleasant talk the servants were assembled, the chapter of God's word was solemnly read, the hymn was chanted, the prayer of praise and thanksgiving was offered, and we were conducted to our place of repose.", "It is not my purpose here to attempt a portrait of those noble friends whom I met then for the first time. The honor of writing the biography of Robert Stuart should be assigned to an abler pen than mine. Everyone who has enjoyed the happiness of his acquaintance, or better still a stay under his hospitable roof, will carry the impression of him to their latest hour — his noble bearing, his genial humor, his untiring benevolence, his upright and uncompromising adherence to principle, his ardent philanthropy, his noble disinterestedness. Irving in his Astoria, and Franchere in his Narrative, give many striking traits of his early character, along with events of his history of a thrilling and romantic interest. But both of them have left the most valuable portion unsaid: his after-life, that is, as a Christian gentleman.", "Of his beloved partner, who survives him still, mourning on her bereaved and solitary pilgrimage, yet cheered by the memory of her long and useful course as a Mother in Israel, we will say no more than to offer the incense of loving hearts, and prayers for the best blessings from her Father in heaven."],
  s4: ["Michilimackinac. That gem of the Lakes. How bright and how beautiful it looked when we walked out the following morning. The rain had passed off and left everything glittering in the light of the sun as it came up over the waters of Lake Huron, far away to the east. The lovely bay lay in front of us, not quite tranquil yet after the storm, dotted over with canoes and with the boats of the fishermen, who were already out getting their nets down for the trout and the whitefish, those treasures of the deep. Along the beach were scattered the wigwams, the lodges of the Ottawas who had come to the island to trade. The people inside them came out to look at us. Then a shout of welcome went up, because they had recognized Shaw-nee-aw-kee, who from a seven years' residence among them was well known to every single one of them.", "A shake of the hand, and an emphatic \"Bon-jour — bon-jour.\" That is the customary salutation between the Indian and the white man.", "\"Do the Indians speak French?\" I asked my husband.", "\"No, this is a fashion they have learned of the French traders during many years of intercourse.\"", "The greeting of every Canadian engagé was just as hearty. Each one trotted forward in his turn to pay his respects to Monsieur John and to deliver a long string of felicitations in a patois I could make nothing of. I was obliged to take on faith all the good wishes being showered on Madame John, of which I understood nothing at all except the hope that I should be happy and contented in my vie sauvage."],
  s5: ["What we had risen early to see was the Mission-house and the school beside it, planted here a few years before by the Presbyterian Board of Missions. Mr. and Mrs. Stuart cared about that place more than about anything else on the island, and they had reason to be pleased with it that morning: it was thriving, and it seemed to promise a great deal of usefulness in the years ahead. They had lived here a long while. They had seen what the island used to be — worldly, dissipated, given over to itself — and they had seen it turned around under God's blessing on Christian effort until you could nearly say of it that \"Religion was every man's business.\" The mission was the beloved child of the handful of Protestant families gathered about it, and the thing they all had in common. Mr. and Mrs. Ferry ran it with zeal and good sense, the congregation kept it warm with encouragement, and its name stood high. It was pleasant to watch what teaching and religion did to the children: how they took the mischief and the trickiness out of the half-breed, and how they stirred the genuine Indian out of his stolid apathy.", "Those were the palmy days of Mackinac. The island held the head-quarters of the American Fur Company, and it was the entrepôt for the whole Northwest. Everything moving in either direction — supplies and trade goods going out, furs and the products of the Indian country coming back — passed through the parent house here or through one of its many outposts, strung along Lake Superior and Lake Michigan, down the Mississippi, and off into country farther away than that.", "Most people know that from the time the old French possessions passed to the British Crown, every Indian tribe but the Miamis and the Wyandots held firmly to the British side. Our winning independence changed nothing in that. Our Government could neither charm their friendship nor buy it. The British, to be fair about it, bid high to keep them. Year after year the leading men of the Chippewas, the Ottawas, the Pottowattamies, the Menomonees, the Winnebagoes, the Sauks and Foxes, and of tribes living farther off still, made the long journey from their own country to Fort Malden in Upper Canada to collect the yearly presents of their Great Father over the water. Keeping them on the payroll was a masterly piece of policy, and in the last war between the two countries it let the men who practised it do fearful execution with such allies at their back.", "What was handed out was worth having: blankets, broadcloths and strouding, calicoes, guns, kettles, traps, silver-work of every sort — arm-bands, bracelets, brooches, ear-bobs — looking-glasses, combs, and a hundred other trinkets besides, and nobody counted them out with a mean hand.", "To the magazines and store-houses of the Fur Company here at Mackinac came all the upper tribes, to sell what they had and to buy what they wanted, the same goods named above and ammunition as well. Ammunition their British friends very commendably neglected to furnish, along with money and liquor.", "They brought furs first, of many kinds, and some of them worth a great deal — beaver and otter, marten, mink, silver-gray fox and red, wolf, bear, wild-cat, musk-rat, and smoked deer-skins. They brought maple-sugar in quantity, and a good deal of Indian corn and petit-blé,[1] and beans, and the folles avoines,[2] which is wild rice. The squaws put in their own share of the merchandise: moccasins, hunting-pouches, mococks — little birch-bark boxes worked over with porcupine-quills and packed with maple-sugar — matting of a neat and lasting weave, and small toy models of Indian cradles, of snow-shoes, of canoes, and of anything else you please.", "In those years it was nothing out of the ordinary to look out and count a hundred canoes or more coming in on the island at one time, every one of them loaded with goods for trade. Set beside that the squadrons of big Mackinac boats arriving without pause from the outposts, carrying the furs and peltries and buffalo-robes that the distant traders had gathered up, and you begin to see the size of what the American Fur Company was doing here, and how many human lives, near and far off, hung on some thread of it.", "It is no wonder that a philanthropic mind, looking out over all these races of uncultivated heathen, should reach ahead in imagination to the day when the white man, spending his energies without rest and sacrificing self and fortune without stint, should see his red brethren climb the scale of social civilization — when Education and Christianity would walk together and make \"the wilderness blossom as the rose.\"", "Not one of those good souls, glad that morning over what their work at Mackinac had accomplished, guessed what was coming. In under twenty-five years there would be nothing left of all these numerous tribes but a few scattered bands, squalid, degraded, hardly a trace remaining of the lofty character they once had. Their lands would be talked out of them or torn out of them. The graves of their fathers would be turned up by the ploughshare. They themselves would be pushed off toward the setting sun, farther with each removal, until the earth itself was literally begrudged them as a place to rest on.", "We could not stay long at the school. The Henry Clay was to sail at two o'clock, and before that we meant to see something of the village and the country around it and then to dine with an old friend of my husband's, Mr. Mitchell. Walking at our ease along the white gravel of the road, I had the houses of the old inhabitants pointed out to me one after another. One was the home of Madame Laframboise, an Ottawa woman. Her husband had taught her to read and write, and from that time on she had used what he gave her for the good of the young people of her own nation. Every day she took a class of them into her house and taught them those branches, and with them the principles of the Roman Catholic religion, which she held to with her whole heart. She had a great deal of energy and enterprise in her, and she was tall, commanding, and dignified in the way she carried herself. Her husband had been killed at his post, off in the trading country, by a Winnebago named White Ox. After that she went out to the posts herself, kept the clerks and engagés under her eye, and made certain with her own judgement that the business was being run in a regular and profitable way.", "The Agency-house stood at the foot of the hill the fort was built on, and it had a piazza and gardens, luxuries you did not expect to find here. It was a lovely spot, for all that everything under cultivation in this cold northern latitude comes up stunted and dwarfish.", "Near it stood the buildings the Fur Company's officials used, a rickety and primitive-looking collection that did no great credit to the architectural skill of my husband. He had overseen the building of them, he told me, when he was hardly more than a boy.", "Then there were the houses of the Dousmans, the Abbotts, the Biddles, the Drews, and the Lashleys, running away along the base of that beautiful hill, and above them all the white walls and buildings of the fort. The climb up is so steep that on the sheer face of it nearest the beach they have built staircases to get from the bottom to the top."],
  s6: ["My head hurt in a way that made the daylight unwelcome — the boat's motion of the day before, still working in me long after I had stepped off it. I did not want to give in to it. So I let myself be walked past every part of the little settlement that could be shown in the short time we had, and then we went to Mr. Mitchell's.", "Mrs. M. received us: very pretty, fine-boned, delicate as glass, French on one side and Sioux on the other, and raised at Prairie du Chien on the Mississippi. At Fort Crawford she had been the belle the young officers arranged their evenings around — so much so that when the post-surgeon killed himself, people laid it to an attachment he had formed for her and she had not returned. What held me was not that story. It was the softness of her manners, and the way her voice rose and fell as though it were half sung, a music I came to know afterward as the mark of every woman in whom French and native blood ran together.", "Another woman was of the company, a lady then staying at the Mission. She saw my head before I had said anything about it, made me lie down on the sofa, and was kinder to me than I had any claim on. She sat beside me and talked of the new sphere opening in front of me, and then she asked:", "\"Do you not realize very strongly the entire deprivation of religious privileges you will be obliged to suffer in your distant home?\"", "\"The deprivation,\" I said, \"will Surely be great, but not entire, for I shall have my Prayer-Book, and, though destitute of a church, we need not be without a mode of worship.\"", "In the years after, alone in the wilderness with that precious book open on my knee and nothing else at hand to comfort me, I thought back on that conversation more times than I can count, and blessed God that so long as I kept it I could never be without \"religious privileges.\"", "We had not yet left the dinner-table when the little steamer's bell went, calling us aboard. There was no time for a proper leave-taking. We said what we could to all those kind friends, and carried away with us their hearty wishes for a safe and prosperous voyage.", "There is hardly a finer thing to look at than Mackinac from the water. The shore let go of us and the whole island arranged itself in one view: the beach sloping down, wigwams scattered along it, canoes drawn up here and there, the odd irregular houses, the white walls of the fort above them, and higher still a farther eminence carrying the remains of old Fort Holmes. Seen whole and complete, it made the outline that had given the place its first name — Mich-i-li-mack-i-nac, the Big Turtle.", "And the water under us. Living water, so clear that the fish showed plainly in it, gliding and darting and turning back; drop a thing over the side and you can follow it down fifty or sixty feet to the bottom, a dollar lying on the green floor of the lake and looking no bigger than a half dime. I have never been able to think the enthusiastic lady foolish who cried, \"Oh! I could wish to be drowned in these pure, beautiful waters!\"", "Off the extreme western point of the island my husband turned me toward the northwest and showed me a promontory a long way out. Point St. Ignace, he said, and told me what it was: one of the earliest white settlements anywhere on this continent. The Jesuit missionaries had a church and a school standing there in 1607, the same year that white men settled St. Augustine in Florida, and one year before anyone laid out Jamestown in Virginia.", "Of everything those devoted men built, nothing is standing now. What is left of them is the memory of the work, and in most cases nothing but the names of the places where they spent their love on their savage brethren. One building survives: the little French church at Sandwich, across from Detroit, the single witness left of the zeal and self-sacrifice of those pioneers of Christianity.", "We passed \"Old Mackinac\" on the main land, which makes the southern edge of the straits, and then the shore opened and let us out onto the broad water of Lake Michigan. Everyone who travels here knows what was done at that old fort, and so does everyone who has read our history: how in Pontiac's day a game of ball was got up outside the walls, friendly enough to watch, so that the officers and the soldiers came out to see it played; how the ball was tossed, very neatly, over the wall; how the players poured in after it as though that were all they were after, and took possession, and massacred the garrison.", "Far to the south the little Indian village of L'Arbre Croche caught the last of the setting sun and glittered. Nothing else on that whole enormous wooded coast gave any sign that a person lived there. Gigantic forest-trees came down to the edge, and here and there a small glade of prairie opened out to the water — ground any farmer would have looked at and wanted, fat with promise — and not one footprint of a man anywhere upon it. We left it to its solitude and laid our course westward across the waters."],
  s7: ["The rainy, gusty weather so common to the equinoctial season caught us again before we reached the mouth of Green Bay, and it stayed with us until the night we arrived on the flats, about three miles below the settlement. There the little steamer grounded, fast and hard. Almost everyone aboard preferred braving the elements to staying cooped up in the quarters we had occupied for the past week, so we decided to trust ourselves to the little boat in spite of the wind, and the rain, and the darkness. In due time we reached the shore."],
  s8: ["We arrived at Green Bay at an unfortunate moment. A treaty was being made between the United States Government and the Menomonees and the Waubanakees, so that not only the commissioners with their clerks and officials, but traders, claimants, travelers, and idlers past counting were on the ground. Most of them were congregated in the only hotel the place afforded. It was a tolerably-sized house near the river-side, and when we came into the long dining-room, cold and dripping from the open boat, we were infinitely amused by the motley assemblage inside it. Groups were seated everywhere. New comers like ourselves stood here and there, because there were not seats enough for all who sought entertainment. The landlord sat calm and indifferent through all of it, his hands in his pockets, exhibiting the full phlegm of a Pennsylvania Dutchman.", "His fat, notable spouse was trotting round the room — stopping to scold about somebody who, burn his skin, had fallen short in his duty, then laughing good-humoredly until her sides shook at some witticism addressed to her.", "She welcomed us very cordially. But when we asked, \"Can you accommodate us?\" her reply was, \"Not I. I have got twice as many people now as I know what to do with. I have had to turn my own family out of their quarters, what with the commissioners and the lot of folks that has come in upon us.\"", "\"What are we to do, then? It is too late and stormy to go up to Shanty-town to seek for lodgings.\"", "\"Well, sit you down and take your supper, and we will see what we can do.\"", "And she did actually contrive to find a little nook, in which we were glad enough to take refuge from the multitudes around us.", "A slight board partition was all that separated us from the apartment occupied by General Root of New York, one of the commissioners of the treaty. The steamer we had come on had brought the mail, in those days a rare blessing to the distant settlements. All the dispatches the General received had to be opened and read through before he could retire to rest, and he received them about bed-time. His eyes were weak, so his secretaries were employed to read the communications to him. He was also a little deaf. Through that slight division between the two apartments the contents of the letters and his comments on them were unpleasantly audible, as he kept admonishing his secretary to raise his voice.", "\"What is that, Walter? Read that over again.\"", "We coughed. We hemmed. We knocked over sundry pieces of furniture. It was no use — they were too deeply interested to hear anything that passed around them, and if we had been politicians we should have had all the secrets of the working-men's party at our disposal, out of which to have made capital."],
  s9: ["Morning, and rain again. Rain, and rain, and nothing in the world but rain. It made no difference to the gentlemen. They meant to take a small boat out to the steamer and bring the luggage up whatever the weather, and among that luggage — the part of it no one could afford to lose — were several boxes of silver, the annuity money for the Winnebagoes at the Portage.", "I went out onto the piazza with some of the others to watch them go. Somebody pointed across the water at Fort Howard, set on a jutting point of the far shore about three-quarters of a mile off: the old barracks, the picketed inclosure, the walls, all of it odd to my eye, and looking ancient and venerable although in fact it had been thrown up only lately. Then we all turned and found the boat again. It had the middle of the river by then. One of the passengers was standing upright in the stern, giving directions, as far as we could tell.", "\"That is rather a venturesome fellow,\" said one, \"if he isn't careful he will lose his balance.\" The words were barely out before the man went over backward — a clean somersault — and was gone into the water.", "\"Oh!\" cried I, \"he will be drowned!\"", "The gentlemen laughed. \"No, there he is, they are helping him in again.\"", "The boat came about at once and brought the party back to shore. I watched them land, and it was not until one of them stepped out and came toward me streaming wet and laughing that I understood I was looking at my own peculiar property. He chose to treat the whole business as a joke. I thought it a rather sad beginning to our Western experience.", "He let himself be talked into leaving his effects in the care of his friends. He changed his clothes and was settling in to stay quietly with me when a vehicle drew up at the door, and out of it came a face we both knew and were glad of — our old friend, Judge Doty.", "Word of our arrival had reached him, and he had come to carry us off at once to his hospitable mansion. We wanted nothing better. We gathered up our bags and our traveling-baskets and went with him without any further ceremony.", "The drive took us first along the edge of Navarino and then through Shanty-town — of the two names, only the second has been honestly earned — through mud and mire, over roads that were barely roads, up and down pitches steep enough to break a neck, until we came to our friends' little brick house. Mrs. Doty took us in with a kindness that was plain sisterly truth, and there was so much welcome in the place that we were at home in it before we had our wraps off.", "They had been expecting us, and the invitations were already written out. The whole circle of Green Bay society was to be assembled to meet us at an evening party. In a new country that is simply the established way of doing honor to a guest or a stranger.", "We asked after Captain Harney, who had kindly offered to bring a boat and a crew of soldiers down from Fort Winnebago to carry us up to that place, our destined home. He had not yet arrived. That left us free, and we made our arrangements for a few days of social enjoyment at \"the Bay.\"", "People shut away as these were from the world at large were hungry for everything we had brought with us: the news, the particulars of how life was being lived and how people were behaving, the thousand small items no newspaper of that day dreamed of furnishing, the fashions, and the general gossip that a lady is erroneously thought to be more au fait of than a gentleman.", "I remember that day chiefly as one nearly unbroken stream of talk, given and taken, with hardly a pause anywhere in it. Every one of the party except me had already made the trip — the voyage, rather — up the Fox River and down the Wisconsin to the Mississippi.", "The anecdotes came mostly out of one journey in particular, made by the three of them in company with a French trader and his two sisters, who were then making their debut as Western travelers. There was Mademoiselle Julie, who would help herself, without asking anybody's leave, to a fine damask napkin or two for wiping out the ducks before they went to the fire. There was the impossibility of persuading either sister that the table apparatus ought to be washed and rinsed nicely before it was packed away in the mess-basket, so that at every meal another good napkin had to be whisked out to wipe the dishes with. And there was the young gentleman's answer to it all, which was to hunt his misappropriated articles out of every nook and corner of the boat, tie them up with a cord, and hang them over the stern to make their own way down the Wisconsin to Prairie du Chien.", "Best of the lot was the story of M. Rolette himself. Somewhere on the route — crossing Winnebago Lake, I believe it was — the travelers fell in with one of the Company's boats headed down to Green Bay for supplies. M. Rolette was one of the Company's agents and the men in that boat were his employés, and he had been some weeks away from home. To meet like that on those lonely waters and trade the news was an event, and both parties felt it as one.", "The boats were stopped. Greetings, in earnest. Then the questions, each one crowding the last.", "\" Eh bien - have they finished the new house?\"", "\" Oui, Monsieur.\"", "\" Et la cheminée, fume-t-elle? \" (Does the chimney smoke?)", "\" Non, Monsieur.\"", "\"And the harvest - how is that?\"", "\"Very fine, indeed.\"", "\"Is the mill at work?\"", "\"Yes, plenty of water.\"", "\"How is Whip?\" (his favorite horse.)", "\"Oh! Whip is first-rate.\"", "Store, farm, the business in every branch of it — the whole of it gone through and settled to his satisfaction, with nothing left to ask. No reason to hold the boats any longer. Time to be off.", "\" Eh bien - adieu! bon voyage! \"", "\" Arrachez, mes gens! \" (Go ahead, men!)", "Then suddenly - \" Arrêtez! arrêtez! \" (Stop, stop!)", "\" Comment se portent Madame Rolette et les enfans? \" (How are Mrs. Rolette and the children?)", "The day wore itself out at last, excitement and all, and we went to our rest — thankful above everything else that General Root and his secretary were not lying a foot from our bed's head with their budget of political news."],
  s10: ["Sleep took me, but it did not keep me. At the first thin grey of dawn something came up to me through the floor, out of the room beneath ours — a chant, low and level, one figure of melody turned over and over, lifting and then sinking again into a kind of grieving. It did not sound to me like anything a living person would make. Wild. Strange. Past all accounting for. I put my hand on my husband and woke him in real fear, and he told me, quite calmly, that what I was listening to was the Indians greeting the opening day.", "Some Menomonees had been given shelter for the night in the kitchen below us, a kindness offered and a kindness taken; and having kept their custom, which never varies, of singing their morning hymn, they let the sound die away and lay down again to sleep. Their listener overhead did not. I could find nothing in it but beauty — a song of praise raised at daybreak by the untaught sons of the forest. Set that beside the civilized and Christianized world, where so many lie down at night and rise in the morning without one breath of thanksgiving to the Almighty Preserver who keeps them, without so much as a thought of Him who gives His angels charge concerning them. The lesson of that hour has never once faded from me. I have liked to believe that in some of those voices the devotion ran as pure as it runs in the Christian who says the sainted Ken's words, \"Awake, my soul! and with the sun,\" etc.", "Callers came to welcome me to the West. Among them were Mr. and Miss Cadle, who stood then at the very first steps of the work that afterward flourished — the education of Indian and half-breed children. Nothing had been built yet. No school-houses, no chapel. We walked over the ground they had chosen for them, and I listened, and was moved by it, while they laid out everything that was to come: the good that would be done, the success that would crown their efforts, the heathen tamed and brought to their Saviour and to the blessings of civilized life. What followed has shown how little the zeal of a few can do when the cupidity of the many stands against it."],
  s11: ["Our evening party went off as parties do everywhere else. The most interesting feature of it to me, because it was the most novel, was the talk of some young ladies I was introduced to, natives of Green Bay or of its vicinity. Their mother was a Menomonee. Their father was a Frenchman, descended from a settler some generations back, and there is reason to believe that he was a branch of the same family of Grignon to which the daughter of Madame de Sévigné belonged. At least it is said the family holds many old papers and records that would give that impression, though the orthography of the name has changed a little since. However that may be, the Miss Grignons were strikingly dignified, well-bred young ladies, and there was a charm in their soft voices and their original, unsophisticated remarks that a stranger found very attractive.", "They also opened to me a new field of apprehension. I said something about how impatient I was to see my new home, and they looked at me with wonder and exclaimed, \"Vous n'avez donc pas peur des serpens?\"", "Snakes. Was it possible there were snakes at Fort Winnebago?", "\"At the Portage! oh! yes — one can never walk out for them — rattle-snakes — copper-heads — all sorts!\"", "I am not naturally timid. I have to admit, though, that the idea of the serpens sonnettes and the siffleurs was not quite a subject of indifference to me.", "There was one among those young ladies whose tall, graceful figure, rich, blooming complexion, and dark, glancing eye would have distinguished her in any drawing-room, and another whose gentle sweetness and cultivated taste made it a matter of universal regret that she was afterward led to adopt the seclusion of a convent.", "Captain Harney and his boat arrived in due time, and active preparations for the comfort of our trip began under the kind supervision of Mrs. Doty. The mess-basket was stowed with good things of every description — ham and tongue, biscuit and plum-cake — not counting the substantials of crackers, bread, and boiled pork, the last of which a lady was thought to be far too fastidious to touch, even if she were starving in the woods.", "We had engaged three Canadian voyageurs to take charge of our tent, our mess-basket, and matters and things in general. Their business was to cut the wood for our fires, prepare our meals, and give a helping hand to whatever was going forward. A messenger had also been sent up to the Kakalin, or rapids, twenty-one miles above, to notify Wish-tay-yun, the most accomplished guide through the difficult passes of the river, to be ready for our service on a specified day."],
  s12: ["In the meantime we had leisure for one more party, and this one was to be a real Western hop. Everybody will remember that dance at Mrs. Baird's. All the people, young and old, who could be gathered from anywhere throughout the country — or, as the fashion was to express it, on Green Bay — were assembled there. The young officers came up from Fort Howard, looking so smart in their uniforms. Treasures of finery that had gone uncalled-for a long while were brought back into the light. Everybody was bound to do honor to the strangers by appearing in their very best, and the entertainment was to be unequalled by any given before it. The whole house was put in requisition for the occasion. Desks and seats were unceremoniously dismissed from Mr. B.'s office, which formed one wing, to make more space for the dancers. Not only the front portion of the house but even the kitchen was made fit for the reception of company, in case some primitive visitor, as sometimes happened, should prefer to sit down quietly in there and smoke his cigar. In those days that was an emergency you always had to provide for.", "Nothing could exceed the mirth and hilarity of the company. There was no restraint but good manners, no excess of conventionalities — only genuine, hearty good-humor and enjoyment, the kind that pleasant, hospitable people with just enough of the French element in them to add zest to any amusement can furnish, to make an entertainment agreeable. In a country so new, where the number of the company at a social gathering mattered more than the quality, the circle was not always, strictly speaking, select.", "I knew that, so I was more amused than surprised when a clumsy little man with a broad, red, laughing face waddled across the room to where I had taken my seat after a dance, and addressed me in this fashion.", "\"Miss K——, nobody hain't never introduced you to me, but I've seen you a good many times, and I know your husband very well, so I thought I might just as well come and speak to you — my name is A——.\"", "\"Ah! Mr. A——, good-evening. I hope you are enjoying yourself. How is your sister?\"", "\"Oh! she is a great deal worse — her cold has got into her eye, and it is all shot up.\"", "Then he turned full upon a lady sitting near me, radiant with youth and beauty and sparkling with wit and genuine humor.", "\"Oh! Mrs. Beall,\" he began, \"what a beautiful gown you have got on, and how handsome you do look! I declare you're the prettiest woman in the room, and dance the handsomest.\"", "\"Indeed, Mr. A——,\" she replied, suppressing her love of fun and assuming a demure look, \"I am afraid you flatter me.\"", "\"No, I don't — I'm in earnest. I've just come to ask you to dance.\"", "Such was the penalty of being too charming."],
  s13: ["It had been arranged that Judge Doty should come with us in our boat as far as the Butte des Morts. His attendant would be waiting there with horses, to carry him on to Mineral Point, where he was to hold court.", "It was a bright and beautiful morning when we left his pleasant home to begin our passage up the Fox River. Captain Harney meant to stay a few days longer at the Bay, but he came to escort us down to the boat and instal us in all its comforts.", "He helped me along over the ploughed ground and the other inequalities in our way to the river-bank, where the boat lay, and as we went he told me how impatiently Mrs. Twiggs was waiting for me. She was the wife of the commanding officer, and since the past spring she had been the only white lady at Fort Winnebago. She wanted a companion and a friend. We had met in New York shortly after her marriage, so we were not quite strangers to each other. I felt perfectly sure of how it would go. When there were two of our sex there — when my piano was safely landed — when the Post Library we had purchased was unpacked — when everything was fairly arranged and settled, we would be, far away in the wilderness as we were, the happiest little circle imaginable. Every anticipation I had was of the most sanguine and cheerful character.", "What lay waiting for us was a moderate-sized Mackinac boat with a crew of soldiers, and our own three voyageurs besides — a dark-looking structure some thirty feet in length. Amidships was a frame-work of slight posts supporting a roof of canvas, with curtains of the same material that could be let down at the sides and the ends, after the manner of a country stage-coach, or rolled up to let in the light and the air.", "In the middle of this little cabin, or saloon, they set the box containing my piano, and on top of it a mattress, which was to be our divan through the day and our place of repose at night if the weather ever proved too wet or unpleasant for encamping. The boxes of silver were stowed next — the silver with which my husband was to pay the annuities due his red children by treaty-stipulation. Our mess-basket was put within convenient reach. We had also bought two large square covered baskets from the Waubanakees, or New York Indians, to hold our various necessary articles of outward clothing and bedding, and to serve at the same time as very convenient little work-tables or dinner-tables.", "As a true daughter of New England, I need hardly say I had not forgotten to supply myself with knitting-work and embroidery. Books and pencils went without saying.", "Most of our furniture, along with the various housekeeping articles we had bought in New York and Detroit, was to follow in another boat, in the charge of men whose professed business it was to take cargoes safely up the rapids and on to Fort Winnebago. That was an enterprise requiring some three weeks of time and an enormous amount of labor, so that the owners of the goods transported might count themselves happy to receive them at all in the end, however wet, broken, and dilapidated their condition might be. That was why we took our choicest possessions with us, even at the cost of being a little crowded.", "Until then I had never seen a gentleman wearing a colored shirt. A spotless white collar and bosom was one of those notions that Boston, and New England folks generally, entertained of what was becoming in a gentleman's toilette. Mrs. Cass had laughingly forewarned me that not only calico shirts but patch-work pillow-cases were an indispensable part of a traveling equipment, and, thanks to the taste and skill of some tidy little Frenchwoman, I found our divan-pillows all dressed in the brightest and most variegated garb.", "The Judge and my husband were gay with the deepest of blue and pink. Each of them was provided, besides, with a bright red cap — a bonnet rouge, or tuque, as the voyageurs call it — which, out of respect for the lady, was to be put on only when a hearty dinner, a dull book, or the want of exercise made an afternoon nap indispensable.", "The Judge was an admirable traveling companion. He had lived many years in the country. He had been with General Cass on his expedition to the head-waters of the Mississippi, and he had a vast fund of anecdote about early times, customs, and inhabitants.", "I happen to remember some instances he gave of the way justice was administered in those days.", "There was an old Frenchman at the Bay named Réaume, excessively ignorant and grasping, though otherwise tolerably good-natured. This man was appointed justice of the peace. Two men once appeared before him, the one as plaintiff and the other as defendant. The justice listened patiently to the complaint of the one and the defense of the other. Then he rose, and with dignity pronounced his decision.", "\"You are both wrong. You, Bois-vert,\" to the plaintiff, \"you bring me one load of hay, and you, Crély,\" to the defendant, \"you bring me one load of wood, and now the matter is settled.\" It does not appear that any exceptions were taken to this verdict.", "That anecdote led to another, and the scene of this one was Prairie du Chien, on the Mississippi.", "There was a Frenchman there, a justice of the peace, universally known by the name of Old Boilvin. His office stood just outside the walls of the fort, and it was much the fashion among the officers to lounge in there of a morning, to find sport for an idle hour and to take a glass of brandy-and-water with the old gentleman, which he called taking a little quelque-chose.", "A soldier named Fry had been accused of stealing and killing a calf belonging to M. Rolette, and the constable, a bricklayer by the name of Bell, had been dispatched to arrest the culprit and bring him in for trial.", "While the gentlemen were paying their customary morning visit to the justice, a noise was heard in the entry, and then a knock at the door.", "\"Come in,\" cried Old Boilvin, rising and walking toward the door.", "Bell. — Here, sir, I have brought Fry to you, as you ordered.", "Justice. — Fry, you great rascal! What for you kill M. Rolette's calf?", "Fry. — I didn't kill M. Rolette's calf.", "Justice (shaking his fist). — You lie, you great —— rascal! Bell, take him to jail. Come, gentlemen, come, let us take a leetle quelque-chose.", "The Canadian boatmen always sing while they row or paddle, and nothing encourages them so much as hearing the bourgeois take the lead in the music. If the passengers, and more especially those of the fair sex, join in the refrain, the compliment is greater still.", "Their songs are of a light, cheerful character, generally carrying some little satire or witticism in them, calculated to produce a spirited chorus, and sometimes an uproarious one.", "The song and the refrain are carried on somewhat in the following style:", "BOURGEOIS. — Par-derrière chez ma tante, Par-derrière chez ma tante.", "CHORUS. — Par-derrière chez ma tante, Par-derrière chez ma tante.", "BOURGEOIS. — Il y a un coq qui chante, Des pommes, des poires, des raves, des choux, Des figues nouvelles, des raisins doux.", "CHORUS. — Des pommes, des poires, des raves, des choux, Des figues nouvelles, des raisins doux.", "BOURGEOIS. — Il y a un coq qui chante, Il y a un coq qui chante.", "CHORUS. — Il y a un coq qui chante, etc.", "BOURGEOIS. — Demande une femme à prendre, Des pommes, des poires, des raves, des choux, etc.", "CHORUS. — Des pommes, des poires, etc.", "BOURGEOIS. — Demande une femme à prendre, Demande une femme à, etc.", "And so it goes on until the advice is given, one piece after another — Ne prenez pas une noire, Car elles aiment trop à boire; Ne prenez pas une rousse, Car elles sont trop jalouses.", "And by the time all the different qualifications have been rehearsed and objected to, lengthened out by the interminable repetition of the chorus, the shout of the bourgeois goes up — \"Whoop la! à terre, à terre — pour la pipe!\"", "It is an invariable custom for the voyageurs to stop every five or six miles to rest and smoke, so that this was formerly the way of measuring distances in that country — so many pipes, instead of so many miles.", "The Canadian melodies are sometimes very beautiful, and a more exhilarating mode of travel can hardly be imagined than a voyage over those waters, amid all the wild magnificence of nature, with the measured strokes of the oars keeping time to the strains of \"Le Rosier Blanc,\" \"En roulant ma Boule,\" or \"Lève ton pied, ma jolie Bergère.\"", "The climax of the fun seemed to be a comic piece which, however often it was repeated, never appeared to grow stale. It went somewhat after this fashion:", "BOURGEOIS. — Michaud est monté dans un prunier, Pour treiller des prunes.", "La branche a cassé — CHORUS. — Michaud a tombé?", "BOURGEOIS. — Ou est-ce qu'il est?", "CHORUS. — Il est en bas.", "BOURGEOIS. — Oh! reveille, reveille, reveille, Oh! reveille, Michaud est en haut! It was always a point of etiquette to look astonished at the luck of Michaud in staying in the tree in spite of the breaking of the branch, and the joke had to be repeated through every variety of fruit-tree that Michaud might be thought capable of climbing."],
  s14: ["By evening of the first day we reached the Kakalin, where another branch of the Grignon family lived, and they took us in and made much of us. I would rather have had the tent pitched on the bank, with the river running past in the dark and nothing at all between me and the beginning of my forest life. I was impatient to set down the indulgences of civilization all at once, the way you set down a coat you have outgrown. But to refuse a house is a slight, and a slight can turn into an affront, so we did the better thing instead. We ate hot venison steaks and crêpes. We drank that cup of coffee no one on earth prepares like a Frenchwoman, the kind that goes all the way through you after a day in the open air.", "The Kakalin is a rapid of the Fox River. It is serious enough that a boat's heavy lading has to be carried around it by land while the boat itself is poled, or dragged on cords, up against the current. It is only the first of a series of rapids and chûtes — falls — strung out between that point and Lake Winnebago, twenty miles above.", "The next morning, after breakfast, we took leave of our hosts and got ready to go on. The bourgeois had been up since an early hour, standing over his men while they worked the boat and its loading across the Kakalin. The late rains had left the paths through the woods and along the banks muddy and poor for walking, so I was put into an ox-cart. The road was unequal the whole way. We saluted, impartially, every stump and every stone that lay in it. The only relief from that came whenever the little thick-headed Frenchman conducting us bethought himself to guide his cattle suddenly into a projecting tree or a thorn-bush, to the great detriment of my straw bonnet, and very nearly of my eyes.", "But we got through at last, and at the head of the rapids I found the boat lying there, everything stowed and ready for us to re-embark.", "Our Menomonee guide came forward to be presented to me. Wish-tay-yun: a fine, stalwart man with an open, good-humored face — roguish, almost, if you watched it long enough.", "\"Bon-jour, bon-jour, maman,\" was his laughing salutation. The French no longer surprised me; I had grown accustomed to that. What surprised me was the respectable title he had decided to bestow on me.", "\"Yes,\" said my husband, \"you must make up your mind to receive a very numerous and well-grown family, consisting of all the Winnebagoes, Pottowattamies, Chippewas, and Ottawas, together with such Sioux, Sacs and Foxes, and Iowas, as have any point to gain in applying to me. By the first-named tribe in virtue of my office, and by the others as a matter of courtesy, I am always addressed as 'father' — you, of course, will be their 'mother.'\"", "Wish-tay-yun and I were soon good friends, my husband turning the Chippewa he spoke into English for me as it came. The morning was already far advanced and we were impatient to be off. Everything was in readiness. The word was given.", "\"Pousse au large, mes gens!\" (Push out, my men.)", "At that moment a boat was seen leaving the opposite bank of the river and making toward us. White men in it. They showed by signs that they wanted us to hold where we were until they could come up. They drew near, and we found them to be Mr. Marsh, a missionary among the Waubanakees, or the New York Indians, lately brought into this country, and the Rev. Eleazar Williams, who was living at that time among his red brethren on the right bank of the Fox River.", "To people situated as they were, even more emphatically than to people in the settlements, the arrival of visitors from the \"east countrie\" is a godsend. We had to give up everything we carried — the news political, the news ecclesiastical, the news social — and then a tolerably detailed account of what we proposed to do, or rather what we hoped we might be able to do, among our native children at the Portage.", "For my own part I was obliged to confess that I was almost entirely a stranger to Indian character and habits, and was going among them with no settled plans of any kind. General good-will, and a hope of making them my friends: those were the only principles I could lay claim to at present. In what way the principle could be carried out for their greatest good, I would have to leave to time and a better acquaintance to show me.", "Mr. Williams was a dark-complexioned, good-looking man. His relations in Connecticut had always spoken of him as \"our Indian cousin,\" so it never occurred to me to doubt that he belonged to that race, although I think now that if I had met him anywhere else I should have taken him for a Spaniard, or a Mexican. His complexion had decidedly more of the olive in it than the copper. His face was grave, almost melancholy. He was very silent the whole of that interview, asking few questions, and offering no observation of any kind except in reply to something put directly to him."],
  s15: ["The rapids took everything the men had. Wish-tay-yun's voice carried over the noise of the water, clear and brassy, the bugle the whole crew worked to; he called, and the rest answered him in one throat and threw themselves at the oars again. Then the current would slew us sideways and the hull would wedge itself between two great stones and stop dead. \"Whoop la! whoop! whoop!\"", "Another haul. Another. Every nerve in them drawn tight, and nothing moved.", "\"She won't budge!\"", "\"Men, overboard!\" The benches emptied before the words were finished, every rower in the river.", "They shoved and hauled and worried her loose, and then walked alongside her in the shallows, steadying her, steering her, until the water ahead flattened into something like calm. Only then did they climb back aboard and take up the oars.", "There were almost no songs that day. Pipes instead, one after another, the only comfort on offer after work like that.", "I had never watched anything of the kind. Whatever I had seen of the world until then, it had not included men driving their bodies to the far edge of what a body will do, and so the sight worked on me harder than it would have worked on someone used to it. The labor alone seemed punishment enough. But to wade waist-deep in cold water, and then to sit down again in soaked clothes with no fire anywhere near them — poor men, it was more than anyone ought to have to bear. Then I saw my husband. After our noon meal he had startled me by appearing in a pair of duck trowsers and a light jacket, and I had not understood why. At the first shout of \"Fast, again!\" he was over the side with the crew, and he \"bore a hand\" through the whole rest of that long stretch.", "He came back aboard only to take the oar of a boy — one of the company of soldiers, slight and fine-boned, who had bled from the nose since the first day at any effort beyond the ordinary. I asked about him, and was not surprised by the answer: a recruit, only just entered the service. He went by the name of Gridley, though I have no doubt that was a name he had chosen rather than been given. Everything about him said he had been raised gently and had enlisted without the least idea what a soldier's life would ask of him. You could read it in him afterward, too, in the discontent he never once put down, until at last he deserted his post. That was some months after the days I am describing here. They took him once and held him a while in confinement, and the moment they released him he went again. His remains were found the following spring, not many miles from the fort. Cold, or starvation; no one could say which. A grim thing to set down — I will go back to the boats."],
  s16: ["Thirteen miles was all the river had given us since the Kakalin, and we had hauled and strained and paid for every one of them. The light was nearly gone by the time the Grande Chûte came into view, and it was there, close under it, that we were to sleep.", "We had already put the Little Chûte behind us — the place where the town of Appleton stands today — and I noted nothing whatever about it except the sheer quantity of extra labor it cost us to fight that rushing water and come out of the fight the winners.", "The last of the sun lay along the high wooded banks, and down between them the Chûte came white and foaming and loud. The men swung the boat toward a small headland on the left bank, chosen because a long level strip ran back from it, room enough and to spare for a good camp. I was over the side before they had fairly pulled her up, and with the Judge to steady me I went as quickly as I could manage to a point farther down the river, where he promised the Chûte showed itself best. I wanted a sketch of it. I wanted it before the daylight was entirely gone, and there was not much of the daylight left.", "The left bank stood to the west, and where it dipped lower than the rest the parting rays came through and fell on our little scene: the boat, the men in their red caps and belts, the two tents already standing. Smoke was just beginning to lift off the evening fires. Behind all of it rose the high wooded bank with the steep portage path barely traceable up its face, and beyond that, farther off and already darkening under the overhanging forests, the long stretch of the fall. It made a lovely landscape. Only an artist's pencil could have done it any justice.", "It was my first encampment, and the newness of every single thing in it enchanted me.", "The fires were small ones, saplings and underbrush gathered in a hurry; the weather was mild enough that we wanted nothing beyond what would cook a supper and dry the men's clothing. The soldiers had pitched their tent some way off from ours — not so far, though, that we could not hear them perfectly well, laughing, enjoying themselves, after a day that had used them all up.", "Corporal Kilgour kept a careful eye on them, and their high spirits never once crossed out of respectful propriety. Our own supper was cooked in the open air with a tea-kettle and a frying-pan and nothing else at all, and by the time it was eaten there was not one of us who was not ready to lie down.", "What woke us at the first grey of the next morning was the bourgeois, calling across the camp, a call built to carry:", "\"How! how! how!\" — uttered at the very top of his voice.", "Nobody sleeps through that. The men come out of their tents, or pick themselves up off the ground beside the fire, and begin turning themselves toward the duties of the day.", "The fire is fed. The kettles go on to boil. The mess-baskets are opened and enough is lifted out of them to make a breakfast. One of the Frenchmen spreads our mat inside the tent — the bedding is already up and folded and waiting to be stowed in the boat — and the tin cups and the plates are set out around the new-fashioned table-cloth. The dew is too heavy this morning for us to eat outdoors; on a drier one the whole business would be laid out on the green grass instead. Then, faster than seems possible, the coffee is steaming in front of us and the ham is broiled, and now and again there is toast besides, brought hot and fresh off the glowing coals.", "Say what you please: there is no breakfast anywhere on earth to equal a breakfast in the woods, with a well-trained Frenchman for master of ceremonies.", "What the men turned to that morning was hard labor — getting the heavy boat up the Chûte. The piano was judged safest left where it stood on board, but everything else had to come out and go up the steep bank by hand, then along the top of it for some hundreds of rods, to the smooth water on the far side, where all the difficulties of our navigation came to an end.", "The Judge kindly took me off my husband's hands while \"the bourgeois\" superintended that important business, and between reading and sketching and wandering about, the morning slipped away from me. Twelve o'clock came, and the preparations for starting were still not finished.", "I was out hunting the finest of the wild flowers, meaning to have a bouquet made up before my husband came back, when I walked into the soldiers' camp-fire. A tall young man was minding a kettle over it — red-faced, light-haired, in fatigue dress — and whatever was in that kettle smelled very good indeed.", "He noticed me watching him work, and he ladled out a tin-cupful of it and politely held the cup toward me.", "I said no, and thanked him, and told him our own dinner would be ready directly.", "\"They left me here to get their dinner,\" he said — he seemed glad enough of somebody to talk to — \"and I thought I might as well make some soup. Down on the German Flats, where I come from, they always like soup.\"", "\"Ah! you are from the German Flats - then your name must be Bellinger or Weber.\"", "\"No, it isn't - it's Krissman.\"", "\"Well, Krissman, how do you like the service?\"", "\"Very well. I was only recruited last summer. I used to ride horse on the Canawl, and, as I can blow a horn first-rate, I expect I will soon be able to play on a bugle, and then, when I get to be musician, you know, I shall have extra pay.\"", "I had not known it, but I made all the proper pleased noises anyway, and left him to his kettle with every good wish for his dreams of ambition — though ambition is not quite the word for them. Avarice is nearer the mark. It was plainly the extra pay he had his eye on, and not the fame. Then I went back to my own quarters.", "My husband has French tastes, and he was a little cast down when I told him what had happened and what I had turned away. We were both repaid soon enough. The tall awkward figure came over to us carrying his kettle, set the composition down in front of the two gentlemen, and had the infinite satisfaction of hearing it pronounced excellent.", "When everything was at last in readiness, the tents came down and went around the Portage, and my husband, the Judge, and I followed after them at our own pace.", "The woods were brilliant with flowers. It was late enough in the season that the glory of the summer was nearly behind us, and still the lupin and the moss-pink and the yellow wallflower were out, and with them every variety of the helianthus, the aster, and the solidago. The gentlemen pulled down clusters of the bittersweet (celastrus scandens) from the boughs overhead and worked them into a wreath for my hat as we went, and the tangled path we went by was Christabel's own, and like hers it was \"Now in glimmer and now in gloom,\"", "through one open glade and then a shady thicket and then another glade. And soon, like that same lovely heroine, \"We reached the place - right glad we were,\"", "and nothing kept us then. We were aboard the little boat again and skimming out over water that had gone perfectly placid."],
  s17: ["The camp we made that night was the finest of the whole journey. The men had been worked hard before noon, so we took only a short stage after it — seven or eight miles of pulling, no more — and put in a little below the \"little Butte,\" where the timber opened into a clearing that might have been cut for us.", "The soldiers went at encamping now like men with a trade. They felled a big tree for the fire and set about cooking food enough for the next day's run, which was to be a long one: twenty-one miles, straight across Winnebago Lake. Our Frenchmen did the same. Then the fire found the light dry grass we were sitting in the middle of, and in a moment the ground all around us was lit and crackling.", "The wind was steady enough to carry the flames one way only, and there was too little to burn for anybody to be frightened by it. We climbed onto the fallen logs and gave that small blaze the grand title of \"a prairie on fire.\" The real loss was that it took every blade of dry grass with it. The night before I had pushed armfuls of that grass under the bear-skin in my tent, and it had made all the difference to my rest.", "Supper was a happy business that evening, eaten at the sunset hour under the high trees, with the water going by and murmuring to itself. Later the moon came up broad and white and poured its light over the whole of it, a country wild and empty and beautiful in its emptiness, and it seemed to me that the friends I had left behind had better reason to envy me than to pity me.", "But nothing keeps, not even that, and at last my raptures over the scenery ran out with everything else. I shut my tent, unwillingly, and lay down close against the fall of the canvas, where I could lift it a little and look out at the night whenever I chose.", "After a time the camp went quiet. The men had stopped talking and seemed to have gone to their blankets, and we were on the very edge of sleep when a heavy step came up outside, and then a blunt voice with it.", "\"Mr. Kinzie! Mr. Kinzie!\"", "\"Who is there? What is it?\"", "\"I'm Krissman, didn't you mean, sir, that the men should have any liquor tonight?\"", "\"Of course I did. Hasn't Kilgour given out your rations?\"", "\"No: he says you didn't say anything particular about it, and he wasn't coming to ask you if you forgot it, but I thought I wouldn't be bashful - I'd just come and ask.\"", "\"That is right. Tell Kilgour I should like to have him serve out a ration apiece.\"", "\"Thank you, sir,\" in the cheerfullest voice imaginable, \"I'll tell him.\"", "Krissman was becoming a character among us.", "A few miles of rowing the next morning brought us to Four-Legs' village, at the mouth of Winnebago Lake — a handsome scatter of Indian lodges standing about on a green glade under tall, fine trees.", "We were properly in the Winnebago country now. The odd name of the place, I soon learned, came from the head chief of the nation, whose home it was. Nobody was there; they had gone off, in all likelihood, to their wintering grounds. Here we parted with our friend Wish-tay-yun, whose own country ended where this one began.", "\"Bon-jour, Chon!\" (John:) \"bon-jour, maman.\" A hard shake of the hand and that was his goodbye. We pushed off into the lake and left him smoking his kin-nee-kin-nick, waiting for the spirit to move him before he set out at his long Indian trot for home in the Menomonee country.", "Our good weather seemed to leave with him. The sky, clear and mild for days, closed over, and instead of the pleasant crossing we had promised ourselves on that silver water we stayed shut up under canvas, putting our heads out now and then to catch what we could of the country through the pouring rain.", "It was no more than an October day owed us, but we were unreasonable enough to find it tedious. To pass the hours, and to make my disappointment a little smaller, my husband told me something of his early life — a story that belonged, as it happened, to the name Four-Legs.", "He was living then at Prairie du Chien, in the service of the American Fur Company. The chiefs and their people came down from the Upper Mississippi to sell their furs and peltries and to buy what they wanted — merchandise, ammunition, trinkets, and the rest.", "Like everyone not yet seasoned to that country, he took the chills and fever. One day, in a fit of the fever worse than any before it, a chief of the Four-Legs family — brother to the one whose village we had just passed — came into the Company's warehouse to trade. There is no ceremony among the Indians, and no restraint either. Hearing that Shaw-nee-aw-kee was sick, Four-Legs went straight to him to say how sorry he was and to tell him what he ought to be taking for it.", "Anyone who has had the ague, and the fever that comes up behind it, knows what it is to be talked at by a man who cannot see that he should stop. The longer the old chief went on, the higher the fever climbed, and the worse the ache grew in his head and his back and his limbs.", "At last the old man arrived at his point. It was not good, he said, for a young man to be sick and far from his family with no home and no wife. He had a daughter at home, a fine one — handsome, healthy, a capital nurse, and the best hand in the whole tribe at trapping beaver and musk-rats. He would be coming down again in the spring, and he would bring her with him, and then Shaw-nee-aw-kee could see for himself that nothing had been overstated. Should he go now, and bring his daughter next time?", "Beaten flat by that babble, wanting nothing on earth but quiet, poor Shaw-nee-aw-kee agreed to all of it eagerly, and the chief went away.", "The fever ran so close to delirium that the whole conversation went out of his head and stayed out for months. It came back to him on the day Four-Legs walked in with a woman well past her youth and, for ugliness, a perfect Hecate. She had an enormous pack of furs across her shoulders, and she came at him with a lurching, criss-cross walk and dropped the pack at his feet — which, by Indian custom, announced exactly what she understood herself to be to him.", "The talk with her father came back to him whole, and with it the understanding that he was going to need some skill to get himself out of where he now stood.", "He told one of the young clerks to take up the pack and carry it into the magazine where the furs were stored. Then he went on talking with the chief about matters of no consequence, quite coolly.", "Miss Four-Legs sat a while with a sulky, ill-used look on her, and at last it came out of her. \"Humph! he seems to take no more notice of me than if I was nobody!\"", "He turned to the clerk again. \"Give her a calico shirt and half a dozen bread-tickets.\"", "Her face did not clear. Seeing that he would have to open the subject himself, the father said, \"Well, I have brought you my daughter, according to our agreement. How do you like her?\"", "\"Ah, yes - she is a very nice young woman, and would make a first-rate wife, I am sure. But do you know a very strange thing has happened since you were here? Our father, Governor Cass, has sent for me to come to Detroit, that he may send me among the Wyandottes and other nations to learn their customs and manners. Now, if I go, as I shall be obliged to do, I shall be absent two or three years, - maybe four. What then? Why, the people will say, Shaw-nee-aw-kee has married Four-Legs' daughter, and then has hated her and run away from her, and so everybody will laugh at her, and she will be ashamed. It will be better to take some good, valuable presents, blankets, guns, etc., and to marry her to one of her own people, who will always stay by her and take care of her.\"", "The old man was shrewd, and shrewd enough to see that what remained to him was the best bargain he could strike. It cost a round sum, I have no doubt, to settle the thing to the satisfaction of the injured damsel, though I have never been able to learn how much. What I do know is that the young gentleman took care never again to close a bargain in a fit of the ague. And the lady up on the Mississippi is called by his name to this day, in derision.", "Halfway down the lake we passed Garlic Island, a lovely place saddled with an unlovely name. It belonged, with the village on the shore opposite, to \"Wild Cat\" — a fat, jolly, good-natured man, nothing whatever like the formidable animal he was named for.", "He and his band were away, like their neighbors at Four-Legs' village, so there was nothing to break the sameness of the sail. It was too wet for singing. The men sat in their overcoats looking like drowned chickens, and had to work their oars unusually hard simply to keep warm, which likely spared them some of the dead weight of that cold, raining October day. We, who had nothing to do, felt it more.", "Toward evening the sun came out. We had passed into the Fox River and were coming into that pretty little widening called Butte des Morts Lake, at the far end of which we were to camp for the night.", "The shallows along the shore were green with wild rice, and the gathering of it, just at that season, is one of the great labors of the Indian women. They push their canoes into the thick of the stand, bend the heads down over the side with their paddles, and beat the ripe husks off the stalks into a cloth laid ready in the bottom. Afterward the grain is rubbed to free it from the husk, fanned in the open air, put into cordage bags and packed away for the winter. It is longer and more slender than Carolina rice, greenish-olive in color, and though it makes a pleasant enough dish it is far from nutritive. They are fond of it as a soup, with birds or venison added."],
  s18: ["The earth, the trees, and the shrubbery were all too full of the heavy rain that had fallen for us to think about encamping, so we made our arrangements to bestow ourselves in our little saloon for the night. Getting a fire lit was a difficult matter. But in the underbrush of a wild, undisturbed place there are always some fragments of dried branch, some tufts of grass that the rain has not reached, and with the assistance of the spunk, or light-wood, which travelers always go well provided with, a comforting fire was eventually blazing brightly.", "After such a chilling, tedious day it was pleasant to gather around it, to sit on the end of the burning logs and watch the Frenchmen making our supper — the kettle nestled in a little nook of bright glowing coals, the slices of ham browning and crisping on the forked sticks, or \"broches,\" which the voyageurs cut so dexterously and set around the brands, the savory messes of \"pork and onions\" hissing in the frying-pan, which is always a tempting regale to a hungry Frenchman. Truly, it takes a wet, chilly trip, taken nearly fasting, as ours had been, to let a person enjoy to its full extent that most social of meals — a supper.", "The bright sun set amid brilliant masses of cloud, the kind that are seen only in our Western skies, and it gave promise of a fine day tomorrow. With that comforting assurance we were glad to take our leave of him, and soon after of each other.", "We had hardly roused up the following morning, in obedience to the call of the bourgeois, when our eyes were greeted with the sight of an addition to our company: a tall, stalwart, fine-looking young mitiff, or half-breed, accompanied by two or three Indians. The salutations of the latter to their \"father\" and their new \"mother\" were vociferous and joyous. They were the first Winnebagoes I had seen, and they were decidedly not the finest specimens of their tribe. The mitiff, a scion of the wide-spreading tree of the Grignons, was the bearer of an invitation to us from Judge Law, who was encamped a few miles above with one or two Green Bay friends, to come and breakfast with him in his tent. We had not dreamed of finding white neighbors out here, but our vicinity could be no secret to them, not as long as there was an Indian in the neighborhood. So, delaying only long enough for the soldiers to finish their breakfast, we pushed on for the \"Butte des Morts,\" or, as Mrs. A always persisted in calling it, Betty More's.", "The white tent of the Judge gleamed in the morning sun as we came up toward the little rising ground it stood on. The river was full of canoes, paddled principally by squaws. There were many Indians to be seen on the banks, all of them with their guns and hunting accoutrements, for the air in every direction was full of flocks of teal, which at that season are most abundant and most delicious. The immense fields of wild rice that abound here and in the little lake below make this neighborhood their favorite place of resort in the autumn months. The effect of that nourishing food is to make the flesh of the birds so fat, so white, and so tender that a young sportsman is always cautioned to fire only at the ones flying very low; shot high in the air, they are bruised to pieces by the fall to the ground and rendered unfit for eating.", "We were hemmed in by a little fleet of canoes that surrounded us, the women chattering, laughing, and eagerly putting forward their little wooden bowls of fresh cranberries as an offering of welcome to me.", "I amused myself with tossing crackers to them. Some of them reached their mark, others fell into the water, and then such a scrambling and shouting — hands and paddles all in requisition, and loud was the triumph of whichever woman got to a floating one first.", "Among the Indians with whom Shaw-nee-aw-kee was now shaking hands, and who all seemed to be old friends, were many fine, straight, well-formed figures, every one of them showing a frame capable of enduring fatigue and the hardships of their way of life. One was describing, with a great deal of gesticulation, the abundance of the game in the neighborhood. He seemed greatly delighted at receiving a quantity of ammunition, and he went off with it instantly to make good his boasts in the matter.", "After walking a short way, we reached the tent, where I was introduced to Judge Law and a pleasant little gray-haired French gentleman of the name of Porlier. Several voyageurs and half-breeds were near by, the former busily at work, the latter lounging for the most part, and going through whatever they had to do with a sort of listless indifference.", "The contrast between the all-alive air of the one class and the apathetic manner of the other was quite striking.", "After a short talk among the members of the party, breakfast was announced, and we went into the tent and took our seats on the ground around the Indian mat that supplied the place of a table.", "The post of honor, namely the head of the table, was of course given to me, so that I could not only look around at the whole circle of the company but also enjoy a fine view out the open door of the tent, and take an observation of everything going on at the side-table outside. Judge Doty sat opposite me, with his back to the opening of the tent, and the other gentlemen on either hand. Our waiter was the tall \"mitiff\" who had been the messenger of the morning. He was still in the same garb — calico shirt, bright-colored scarf around his waist, and on his head a straw hat encircled with a band of black ostrich feathers, the usual dress of his class.", "The tin cups that were to hold our coffee were duly set around, then breakfast-plates of the same metal, with knives and forks, and then the viands, among the most conspicuous of which was a large tin pan of boiled ducks.", "The Judge, wishing to show, probably, that although we were in the vast wilderness all fastidious nicety had not been left behind, picked up the plate that had been set in front of him, and, seeing something adhering to it that did not exactly please him, handed it back over his shoulder to Grignon with the request that he wipe it carefully. Grignon complied. He pulled a black silk barcelona handkerchief out of his bosom, where it had been snugly tucked away against any occasion that might present itself, gave the tin a furious polishing, and handed it back again. The Judge looked at it with a smile of approbation and gave a glance around the table, as much as to say, \"You see how I choose to have things done.\" Then he applied himself to his breakfast.", "The trail for Fort Winnebago at that time led from the shore opposite Butte des Morts, through Ma-zhee-gaw-gaw swamp, and past Green Lake, and it was well for the Judge that his horses stood waiting for him to mount and away as early as possible after breakfast — or I am afraid the story I should have been tempted to tell would have made his ride an uncomfortable one all day long.", "We had hardly finished breakfast when our hunter, the one who had received the ammunition, came back bringing about fifty fine ducks, which he had shot in little more than an hour. From that time until the close of our trip, our supply of those delicate birds was never wanting."],
  s19: ["There is a mound at Butte des Morts, and the English of it is the Hillock of the Dead. The name is not ornamental. A battle was fought on that ground long ago between the French and the Mis-qua-kees, whom everyone here calls the Foxes, and the killing ran so far past the ordinary that the rise of earth where the bodies were put down took its name from them and has carried it ever since. Until that day the Foxes had lived along the banks of the river that still goes by their name. They came out of that fight overwhelmed and beaten, and they left the river behind. They went down toward the Mississippi and asked shelter of their allies the Saukies — the Sauks, as they are called now — and year by year the two peoples grew into one another, until the joined tribes came to be known, as they are today, by the single name \"Sauks and Foxes.\"", "The French of the upper country keep a private name for every tribe, each one fastened to some quirk of habit or of body. The Chippewas are light on their feet, so they are \"Sauteurs,\" which is to say Jumpers. The Ottawas are the \"Courtes-oreilles,\" Short-ears. The Menomonees live where the wild rice stands thick, and so they are \"Folles Avoines.\" The Winnebagoes bind the fur of a polecat about their legs when they go out equipped for war, and for that they are \"les Puans.\" The Pottowattamies keep themselves poorly, and are therefore \"les Poux.\" The Foxes are \"les Renards.\" The list runs on in that fashion.", "The consequence is that no French or half-breed resident of the country will name an Indian to you in any other way. \"Such a person is a 'Court-oreille.'\" \"Is that woman a 'Winnebago'?\" \"No, she is a 'Folle Avoine.'\" A newcomer spends his first weeks a good deal puzzled about how to sort the acquaintances he is making.", "Every one of the native friends gathered around us in this place belonged to \"les Puans\" — though the word they use for themselves is far better sounding: the \"Ho-tshung-rahs.\"", "Saying adieu to Judge Doty went hard with me. His company had been the best part of the journey, and his example had taught us a thing worth carrying away, which is to take matters as you find them. We got our leave-taking over not long after breakfast, thanked our kind hosts, and set forward.", "Butte des Morts to the Portage is about seventy miles if you go by land. By water it is not less than a hundred and thirty. The river refuses to run straight — it doubles and coils through the low swampy prairies that lie over so great a part of that country."],
  s20: ["Six miles above the Butte another water comes down to meet the Fox. It is called Wolf River, and it is wide, and it runs straighter and looks more promising than the crooked thing it empties into, which is exactly the trouble with it. Strangers have taken it for the main stream. They have gone up it a long way, hours of good hard rowing, before the country stopped agreeing with what they expected of it and they understood that every one of those miles would have to be undone.", "Past that junction the river begins to play games with the compass. I was always watching the banks for something worth a sketch, and at one bend I found it: a strip of ground close on the water, broad trees leaning out over it, two or three wigwams tucked in under the shade of them. Small children were running about in the sun with nothing on at all. Two women sat on the ground with a half-finished lodge mat across their laps. I got out my pencil and began. The bank saw us then and knew Shaw-nee-aw-kee, and the shout went up as it always did — \"Ee-awn-chee-wee-rah, Hee-nee-kar-ray-kay-noo.\"[12] They were a people who let you know when they were glad, and they let us know. Then we moved on, and I kept working at the drawing.", "The trouble was the sun. It had been squarely behind me when I started. After a while it was off my left shoulder. Then it was straight in front of me. I shifted where I sat, and shifted again, and by and by it had settled on my right hand and stayed there. I could not account for it, and I looked up from the paper to see why. \"Oh, here is a pretty scene. I must have this too! But how surprisingly like the one I have just finished, only in a different direction.\" The laughing came off the bank a second time. It was the same bank. We had rowed the better part of four miles since I had first put pencil to it, and all we had to show for that labor was the width of the little shelf of ground the trees and the lodges stood on. I was a traveler in a hurry. The river was not.", "We came out onto Lake Puckaway late in the evening of our second day from Butte des Morts. A white man named Gleason lived there — the one poor Hooe used to aim his little pun at, since nothing Gleason ever told you stayed the size he had told it: \"All isn't gold that Gleasons.\" We did not go to his house. Late in the season as it was, the mosquitoes along that shore were beyond bearing, and we were glad to put ourselves as far off the bank as the water allowed and make our beds in the boat.", "The lake is named for the flags that grow in it, long rushes standing thick in the shallows, and out of them the women make the coarse matting that covers a lodge. The way of it is old and wants almost nothing. A woman sits on the ground with the rushes laid side by side and made fast at either end. Her shuttle is a long flat needle cut from bone, with a cord of tree bark run through it, and she drives that needle through one rush and then the next until every stalk is bound hard against its neighbor and the whole is one fine solid mat. They are seldom made longer than five or six feet. Anything bigger is unhandy when a lodge is being set up or taken down.", "The work falls to the older women of a family. When a woman has been worn through — when the weather and the hardship have gone on long enough that she can no longer bring down a tree, or hoe the corn, or carry a load on her back — she is set to weaving mats, and to minding the children, and to keeping order among the dogs, of which there is never any shortage in an Indian lodge.", "Our course took us next into Lac de Boeuf, Buffalo Lake, and it is a lovely sheet of water. In places the shore is very fine, with headlands running out into the clear depths and the great stands of timber crowning them lying upside down in the water as if in a mirror. Now and then a deer would cross one of the glades that opened at intervals in the woodland and be gone. A pair of sand-hill cranes got up ahead of us, beating their wings slowly, looking for somewhere quieter to be. Teal came in flocks, skimming low over the surface and then lifting toward the shelter of the forest, and our sportsman felt every one of them; but a bird brought down in that country would most likely never be found again, and he would not give way to the wanton pleasure of killing for its own sake.", "Then the lake let us out and the river had us back, narrow again, doubling on itself again, and with it went the last of anything worth looking at between there and the end of our trip."],
  s21: ["The country gave us seventy miles and nothing at all to look at. The same low ground, the same reeds, the same sky sitting flat on top of it. What sharpened the tedium into something worse was knowing the shape of the alternative: with horses, or a carriage of any kind, we could have gone overland from Gleason's to the Portage through pleasant country in a little more than three hours. Even our one dependable comfort was taken from us. The voyageurs' songs need oars, and the river, deep as it is, runs so narrow that in many places there is no room for the regular play of the oars; and a Frenchman can never keep tune unless his oars can keep time. Lapierre tried. He worked away with a paddle — the little row, he called it — and pitched his voice against the silence, and it would not go. Then there was the wild rice. In places it grows so thick that it closes the water over entirely, and even a moderate-sized boat must be forced through the tangled masses stroke by stroke, the stalks hissing along the hull the whole way. Two days of that, and still the worst of the impatience was ahead of us. It did not arrive until we came in sight of the white walls of Fort Winnebago, looking down from a rising ground over the vast expanse of low land the river winds through.", "The Indians have a tradition about this water. A great serpent once lived in the Mississippi, and took a freak to visit the Great Lakes, and dragged his length across the prairies going; and the trail he left behind collected the water off the meadows and the rain out of heaven as it fell, and in time it became the Fox River.", "The little lakes strung along its course are probably the places where he thrashed about in his uneasy sleep at night. In the neighborhood of the Portage he must have played all the antics of a kitten. When the fort was first pointed out to me I cried out, delighted, \"Oh, we shall be there in half an hour!\"", "\"Not quite so soon,\" my husband said, smiling. \"Wait and see.\" So we sat and watched. We came up on the very bank where we were to disembark — near enough to distinguish the officers standing on it, and a lady with them, waiting to receive us. Then the boat swung, and we had our backs to them, and we were shooting out into the prairie again. After a while another bank came around, with a range of comfortable-looking log houses on it. \"That's the Agency,\" my husband said. \"The largest house belongs to Paquette, the interpreter, and the others are the dwellings of our Frenchmen. The little building, just at the foot of the hill, is the blacksmith's shop, kept there by the Government, that the Indians may have their guns and traps mended free of expense.\"", "\"But are we going to stop there?\"", "\"No — do you not see we are going back to the fort?\"", "And we were. Our course had turned again and we were setting off in our first direction. It went on that way, tacking right and left, putting backward and forward, the walls showing themselves and sliding out of view and showing themselves again, for the better part of two hours, until at last we came in to the little landing, where the assembled party stood ready to greet us."],
  s22: ["Major and Mrs. Twiggs came out to meet us, and with them a few of the younger officers — nearly all the older ones were away — and our brother Robert, who is known to every tribe in the country simply as \"Bob.\" The warmth of that welcome is not a thing I can explain to anyone who has not done what we had just done: travelled a long way to a house set down alone in the wilderness. The Major would not hear of our going to \"the Agency,\" which had been the plan. There were empty quarters inside the fort, and he wanted us in them that hour.", "\"No - we must be under the same roof with them. Mrs. Twiggs had been without a companion of her own sex for more than four months, and would certainly not hear of a separation now. But we must be their guests until the arrival of the boats containing our furniture,\" which was creeping up from Green Bay in the charge of our old acquaintance Hamilton Arndt.", "Dinner was already waiting. That is what the crooked, doubling passage up the Fox River does for you: nobody ever arrives unannounced, because everyone has had days to watch you coming. The whole circle sat down together, and I do not remember a merrier table.", "Afterward Mrs. Twiggs walked me across the wide hall to see the rooms that were to be ours — two large rooms on each of the building's three floors. The front room on the ground floor stood empty. The one behind it was plainly meant for sleeping, since it held a bedstead of such enormous and ungovernable size that Og, the King of Bashan, might have stretched out on it with Mrs. Og and all the little Ogs beside him. We laughed; we could not help it. And the bedstead was the lesser wonder of that room. Something else stood in the second corner.", "It had been raised under the personal direction of one of our young lieutenants, and he and the soldiers who built it had emptied out every drop of architectural feeling they possessed. The timbers were grooved and carved. The pillars holding up the front bulged and narrowed and bulged again in the most imaginative way. The doors were not merely panelled; they radiated, in a manner calculated to stun any eye that had never seen better. One of these had been put up in every set of quarters, to make good the want of closets — a want that had not once crossed the minds of the bachelors who drew the plans until it was far too late to fix. Each structure had three compartments, intended, one supposes, for clothes-press, store-room, and china-closet; those, at least, were the uses Mrs. Twiggs had found for hers. The only flaw was in the last of the three, where the shelves were set so close together that a gravy-boat could not be got in between them, though what they lacked in space they certainly made up in number. In honor of the man who conceived it we named the whole production a \"Davis,\" and so laid the first laurel on a brow that would later distinguish itself in Cabinet making of an altogether different sort.", "Fort Winnebago stood on a bold shoulder of ground. On one side it looked down over the long reach of prairie and the Fox River; on the other the land ran back into a heavily wooded ridge going off toward Belle Fontaine and Lake Puckaway.", "Out in front was meadow, and cut across the meadow ran the Portage road, some two miles of it, joining the Fox to the Wisconsin. The Government kept teams of oxen at the Agency, and a driver with them, for hauling the Indians' canoes over that ground — which through much of the year was soaked and miry and very nearly impossible to cross at all."],
  s23: ["The woods had turned. Every tint of autumn was in them at once, and the ground under them was full of movement — groups of Indians in every direction, lodges pitched here and there around the Agency buildings, smoke going up thin and straight in the cold. On the low ground below stood the white tents of the traders. They had come early and set up already, stocked to furnish the winter supplies, and waiting on the annuity money that was about to pass into Indian hands and straight back out of them again.", "A great concourse had been gathering for days. They were waiting on the payment, and the payment was waiting on Shaw-nee-aw-kee, who was bringing the silver.", "Before any of it could begin, the great chief of the nation had held a carouse. Four-Legs, whose village we had passed at the mouth of Winnebago Lake. It is too apt to be the custom when the tribes come into the neighborhood of a sutler's establishment, and in this case the means were more than usually ready to hand, because there were traders on the ground who held the very stringent laws against selling liquor to the Indians as no particular obstacle.", "Four-Legs could not stand this full tide of prosperity. His Father the agent was not there to check him, and there was nothing else in the way, and he carried his indulgence so far that within a few days it killed him. They had buried him with unusual pomp the day before we arrived, and my chief feeling, when I learned it, was disappointment at having come too late to witness the ceremonies.", "They had wrapped the body in a blanket, as their custom is, and laid it in a rude coffin along with his guns, his tomahawk, his pipes and a quantity of tobacco. Then they carried it up to the highest point of the hill facing the fort, an immense procession of his people coming behind it, whooping, beating their drums, howling — making altogether what is emphatically called a pow-wow.", "When the body was in the ground they planted a stake at its head, painted over in vermilion with a series of hieroglyphics setting out the great deeds and events of his life. Around the whole of it they drove pickets cut from the trunks of tamarack-trees. To that enclosure his friends would come for many days together, to renew the expression of their grief and to throw tobacco and other offerings over the grave to the Great Spirit.", "There was one consolation in having come late. We were still in time to furnish a quantity of white cotton for a flag to wave over the grave, and to settle a considerable bill at the sutler's for the various articles the funeral parade had been found to require — it being a duty expected of their Father to bury the dead suitably.", "The observances were not finished with him. All that day, and then all that night, the sound of their instruments came across to us mixed with lamentation, and with the discordant whoops and yells of men who were still partly drunk, and there was no sleeping through it. Every so often something else threaded through the noise: the plaintive notes of the Indian flute, which a young man plays when he is in love. Grief and whiskey had made their hearts tender, and the woods gave back their melancholy strains until morning."],
  s24: ["Early the next morning, before I had left my room, a sound came through the wall that brought me up out of my chair — a wailing, from the apartment adjoining. When I went in, several squaws were seated on the floor with their eyes down, their whole attitude one of condolence and sympathy, and in the middle of them sat a little ugly woman in tattered garments, her face blackened, her hair loose and wild, sobbing and crying bitterly.", "I had no doubt they were the family of the dead chief, and it troubled me that I could say nothing to them about their sorrow except with my hands.", "Knowing nothing of their customs, I took it for granted from the state of them that poverty was one of the things they were grieving over. One of the party, at the very least, looked to be at the bottom of everything a person can lose. Can it be possible, I said to myself, that this poor creature has nothing but these scanty rags to cover her?", "I stepped back into my own room and brought out a pretty calico wrapper, and put it into the hands of the little dirty blackened object in the middle of the floor. She took it, and began sobbing and sighing all over again. I made signs to her to put it on. I opened it out and showed her how it was to be worn, and urged her, by gestures, to lose no time in making herself more comfortable.", "At this the other women burst out laughing.", "Very mal-à-propos, I thought, and somewhat unfeeling. Then my husband came in and explained to me that the chief mourner was Madame Four-Legs, the widow; that she had, beyond question, a comfortable wardrobe waiting for her at home; and that it is part of the etiquette of mourning to go for a season with the person neglected and the face blackened. All this was told me in the intervals of shaking hands and offering and receiving condolences in the most uncouth, guttural language I had ever heard. Their Father dismissed them at last, with the promise of some presents to help dry their tears. Let no one infer from the calico wrapper that the poor little widow's grief was anything but sincere. She had been greatly attached to her husband, and had held great influence, not with him only but with the nation at large. She was a Fox woman, and spoke Chippewa, which is the court language among all the tribes, so that she was often sent for to interpret, and had been in the habit of traveling with her husband and assisting him with her counsel on every occasion. She was a person of great shrewdness and judgment, and, as I afterward had reason to know, of strong and tenacious affections."],
  s25: ["After breakfast the principal chiefs came to call. They had put on their best clothing and their best paint to look at their new mother.", "There was Naw-kaw, or Kar-ray-mau-nee, the Walking Turtle, now the principal chief of the nation — a stalwart man with a broad, pleasant face, the whole of which was governed by an immense under lip that hung nearly to his chin. There was the old Day-kau-ray, the most noble and dignified and venerable man of his own tribe or of any other I have known. He had a fine Roman face, made stranger and finer by a bald head with one solitary tuft of long silvery hair on it, neatly tied and falling back on his shoulders. His dress was perfectly neat and perfectly appropriate and almost without ornament. His courtesy he never laid aside under any circumstances whatever. All of it together gave him the highest place in the consideration of everyone who knew him. It will be seen further on that his character was not less grand and striking than his person and his bearing.", "There was Black-Wolf, whose lowering, surly face was well described by his name. The fierceness of it was heightened by masses of heavy black hair hanging loose all round it, which is quite contrary to the usual fashion among the Winnebagoes. They generally remove a portion of the hair and draw the rest to the back of the head, where it is clubbed and ornamented with beads, ribbons, cock's feathers — or, if the man is entitled to them, an eagle's feather for every scalp he has taken from an enemy.", "There was Talk-English, a remarkably handsome and powerful young Indian, who came by his name in this way. He was one of a party of sixteen Winnebagoes who had gone, the year before, at the invitation of the Government, with their Agent and with Major Forsyth — the Chippewa, as they called him — to visit the President at Washington.", "On the trip, the question people not familiar with Western Indians naturally put to them was, \"Do you talk English?\"", "The young fellow was very observant, and he came to his Father with it. \"What do they mean by this? Everybody says to me, talk English!\"", "The Agent interpreted the words for him. \"Ah, very well.\"", "The next place they came to was Lockport, in the State of New York. He jumped off the canal-boat onto the lock, ran up to the first man he saw, thrust his face forward into the man's face, and demanded, \"Talk Eengeesh?\"", "\"Yes,\" said the man. \"Do you talk English?\"", "\"Ya-as.\"", "From that day forward he carried the name Talk-English, and it was entered on the pay-rolls, and he was not a little proud of it.", "Hoo-wau-ne-kah, the Little Elk, was another of the distinguished men of the tribe, and had also been at Washington. Henry Clay came to see the deputation there, and looked carefully over the countenances and the bearing of every man in it, and then pointed to Hoo-wau-ne-kah as the one possessing the greatest talent. He was much pleased when they told him he had picked out the principal orator of the nation, a man decidedly superior in ability to any other individual of the tribe.", "Wild-Cat, our Indian Falstaff in everything but the cowardice and the falsehood, I have already spoken of.", "Then there was Kau-ray-kaw-saw-kaw, the White Crow, a Rock River Indian, who afterward distinguished himself as the friend of the whites during the Sauk war. The French called him le Borgne, because he had lost an eye. He wore a black silk handkerchief drooping over the left side of his face to hide the blemish, and that, with the rest of his native costume, gave him a very singular appearance.", "There was a nephew of the dead chief Four-Legs, whom whites and Indians alike, and with perfect justice, called the Dandy. Out of mourning, his dress was of the most studied and fanciful character. A shirt, when he condescended to wear one, of the brightest colors, covered with innumerable rows of silver brooches set close together; never fewer than two pairs of silver arm-bands; leggings and moccasins embroidered in ribbons and porcupine-quills in the most elaborate manner; everything he could devise in the way of ornament hanging from the club of hair behind; a feather fan in one hand, and in the other a mirror, into which he looked at himself every five minutes. Add the variety and brilliancy of the colors on his face — the choosing and laying on of which took up no small part of the hours he allotted to his toilet — and you have the equipment of young Four-Legs.", "All this devotion to dress and appearance seemed not altogether out of place in a youthful dandy. But we had an old one of the same stamp. Pawnee Blanc, the White Pawnee, outdid his younger competitor, if such a thing were possible, in attention to his personal attractions.", "On this occasion he came in all his finery, and went through the customary salutations with an air of solemn dignity, and then walked, as the others did, out of the hall where I had received them and into the parlor, where every one of them sat down on the floor. The room, fortunately, was still bare of furniture. But alas, I thought, for my pretty carpet, if this is to be the way they pay their respects to me. I sat and watched the ash falling from the ends of their long pipes, and the other inconveniences that attend the use of tobacco, or kin-nee-kin-nick, with absolute dismay.", "The chiefs were followed by the interpreter and his wife, and with them all the Canadian and half-breed women whose husbands found employment at the Agency or at the American Fur Company's establishment.", "By that time my piano had been taken out of its case and set up in our quarters. To our great joy it had come through entirely uninjured. Thanks to the skill of Nunns and Clark, not one note was out of tune.", "The women had never seen such a thing in their lives, and they were loud in their exclamations of wonder and delight.", "\"Eh-h-h! regardez donc! Quelles inventions! Quelles merveilles!\" One of them, watching the play of my fingers reflected in the nameboard, called out to her companions in great exultation. She had found, as she believed, the hidden machinery that made the sounds, and she was not a little mortified when we undeceived her."],
  s26: ["The boats might come in any day, so it seemed best to begin at once on whatever preparation for housekeeping was in my power. That amounted to fitting and sewing my carpets, in which Mrs. Twiggs kindly helped me; and the wife of one of our Frenchmen came over from the Agency and made everything tidy and comfortable, so that the carpets were soon tacked down and the rooms stood ready for the rest of the furniture, whenever the river chose to deliver it.", "I had tried a great many times, in Detroit and again in Green Bay, to engage a servant-woman to come with me to my new home, and had failed every time. One would present herself and we would begin to talk terms, and then, before we could come to any final agreement, the thing would break off — the distance did it, or the savages, or the hardships of the trip, or, as likely as not, the objections of her friends. In the end I had to content myself with the simple hope my husband held out, that one of his French employés and his wife might be willing to take up their abode with us.", "That was where matters stood when Major Twiggs removed the difficulty altogether. He proposed that we take into our service a young colored girl he had brought up from Buffalo in the spring, to wait on Mrs. Twiggs until her own servants should arrive from the South.", "So Louisa was sent for. She was an uncommonly handsome young negress with an intelligent face and a very demure one, and she called herself fifteen years of age — though from the progress in vice and iniquity I afterward discovered her to have made, she must have been at least several years older than that. Be that as it may, at the time she seemed to have no fault in her but carelessness and inexperience, and I had great hopes that careful training would get the better of both.", "My first week's visit with Mrs. Twiggs had just run out when the word came that the boats were in sight — the boats with our furniture in them. The expected arrival of Louis Philippe to visit Queen Victoria could hardly have raised a greater sensation in our little community. We knew perfectly well that some hours must pass before they could reach the place of disembarkation, and we watched the whole time anyway; and in the end all the young officers, with as many of the soldiers as were off duty behind them, went down the bank to the landing with Mr. Kinzie, to see everything come ashore and to help it if help was wanted.", "It was a sad plight that matters were found in. As each box was hoisted onto the bank, the water ran out of its corners. Nobody had the patience to wait until they could be carried up to the fort. The gentlemen furnished themselves with hammers and hatchets and fell eagerly to work opening them where they stood, to find out the extent of the damage. Alas for the mahogany. There was not a piece of it whose edges and veneering were not starting away from the wood. It had all the appearance of having lain under the Grande Chûte for days together. Poor Hamilton was loud in his protestations and his excuses.", "It was the fault of the men. It was the fault of the weather. It was the fault of the way the things had been packed. \"Confound it! he had taken the best care of the things he possibly could — better than he had ever taken before — it would get done!\"", "There was nothing to do but be patient and make the best of it. And when the pretty sideboard and the work-table had been thoroughly rubbed and set up, and all the little knick-knacks arranged along the mantel-piece — when the white curtains were hung at the windows, and the chairs and the dining-table each stood in its proper relation to the piano — our parlor was pronounced magnificent. Hamilton certainly thought so. He came to give it one admiring look and to hear the music of the piano, which was a perfect novelty to him. The description he gave of it to the young officers after he got back to the Bay expressed his admiration and his wonder together: \"There it stood on its four legs! Anybody might go up and touch it!\"", "In time the dinner- and tea-sets were carefully bestowed in the \"Davis,\" along with sundry jars of sweetmeats I had put up in Detroit. The iron and tin utensils went into a neat cupboard in the kitchen, of which my piano-box supplied the frame. The barrel of eggs and the tubs of butter, brought the whole way from Ohio, were ranged in the store-room. A suitable quantity of salt pork and flour was bought from the commissary, and there was no lack of game of every description, that being the offering of our red children. We were ready to begin housekeeping.", "The first dinner in her own house is an era in the life of a young housekeeper, and I shall certainly never forget mine. I was down in the lower regions superintending my very inexpert little cook when my husband appeared to tell me that the payment — then the all-absorbing topic of interest — would not begin until the afternoon, and that he had accordingly invited M. Rolette, Mr. Hempstead, and four other gentlemen to dine with us.", "\"So unexpected — so unprepared for?\"", "\"Never mind, give them anything you have. They have been living for some days in tents, and anything will taste well to them.\"", "My dinner had been meant to consist chiefly of a venison pasty, and by great good fortune the only dish among my store large enough for it was of very large proportions indeed, so that there was already smoking in the oven a pie nearly equal in size to the famous Norwich pudding. With a few trifling additions to the bill of fare we made out very well, and the master of the house had the satisfaction of hearing his impromptu dinner very much commended by all six of his guests."],
  s27: ["There were two divisions of the Winnebago Indians. One was paid by the Agent, at the Portage; the other at Prairie du Chien, by General Street. The first of them numbered between four and five thousand people, and under the treaty stipulations they received fifteen thousand dollars a year, along with a considerable amount of presents, and a certain number of rations of bread and pork to be issued in times of emergency through the year.", "The principal villages of this division stood at Lake Winnebago, at Green and Fox Lakes, at the Barribault, at Mud Lake, at the Four Lakes, at Kosh-ko-nong and at Turtle Creek. When the annuity-money came, or a little before it, messengers went out to every one of those villages to notify the heads of families and lodges to assemble at the Portage.", "When they arrive, the masters of families, under their several chiefs, give in their names and the number of people in their lodges to be registered. Since a fixed sum is apportioned to each individual, it is of course an object to a head of a lodge to make his registered number as large as it will go. Each man brings his little bundle of sticks and presents it to the Agent for entry. Sometimes a dialogue like this one follows:", "\"How many have you in your lodge?\"", "The Indian counts his bundle of sticks over carefully, and with great ceremony. \"Fifteen.\"", "\"How many men?\"", "\"Two.\" The Agent lays two sticks aside. \"How many women?\"", "\"Three.\" Three more are separated out.", "\"How many children?\"", "\"Eight.\" Eight sticks are added to the heap.", "\"What is the meaning of these two sticks that stay?\"", "The culprit, whose arithmetic has not carried his deception all the way through, disappears in the shouts and jeers of his companions, who are always well pleased at the detection of any roguery they have had no share in.", "The young officers generally helped count out and hand over the money at these payments, and it was no unusual thing, when the last band came up, for the chiefs to take a quantity of silver out of the box and ask their Father to pay his friends for their trouble. They seemed really disturbed when he refused. In that, as in nearly everything, you see the native courtesy and politeness that they never lose sight of among themselves. If a party comes to their Father to beg for provisions and food is set before them, then however hungry they are, every one of them waits while one of the company divides the whole of it into equal shares; and then each takes his share and eats it quietly, with the greatest moderation. I never saw that rule broken but once.", "Our friend Pawnee Blanc, the Old Dandy, came one day with a party of Indians and asked leave to dance for us in the open space before the door. It was a warm, dusty afternoon, and as our friends grew heated and tired with the violence and length of the exercise, a pitcher of raspberry negus was made and sent out to them. Pawnee took the pitcher and the tumbler. He poured the tumbler about half full and gave it to the first man of the circle, then filled it the same way for the next, and so on down the line — until it occurred to him to look into the pitcher. What he saw in there settled his course of action. He set the tumbler down on the ground, raised the pitcher in both hands to his lips, and took a long, hearty pull; after which he went on down the circle, giving less and less, until he was called in to have the pitcher replenished. Everyone present agreed it was the only instance any of them had ever witnessed of an Indian appearing afraid of getting less of a thing than his share.", "During a payment a good many kegs of whiskey find their way into the lodges, whatever the watchfulness of the officers and the Agent. Where there is a demand there will always be a supply, let the legal prohibitions be what they may. The last day of the payment is, without exception, a day of general carousing.", "When the men begin their frolic, the women go quietly around and gather up the guns, the knives, the tomahawks, every weapon of any description, and hide them all, so that as little mischief as possible can be done in the absence of restraint and reason. I am sorry to have to record that our little friend Pawnee Blanc was greatly addicted to the pleasures of the bottle.", "Among the presents Shaw-nee-aw-kee had brought from the East for the chiefs were a trunk of blue cloth coats trimmed with broad gold lace, and a box of round black hats ornamented in the same way. Anyone who knows Indians of any tribe will have noticed that their first step toward civilization, in man or woman alike, is mounting a man's hat, decorated with tinsel or ribbons or feathers. Pawnee was among the happy number remembered in the distribution, and he put his new costume on at once, tied a few additional bunches of gay-colored ribbons to the long spear that was always his baton of ceremony, and came straight over, followed by an admiring train made up chiefly of women, to pay me a visit of state.", "The solemn gravity of his face as he waved off anyone who came near enough to finger his newly received finery, and the dignity with which he strutted along, edging this way and that to avoid any possible contact with homely, every-day wardrobes, augured well for a long continuance of propriety and self-respect and a due regard for the good opinion of all around him. But alas for Pawnee. Late in the day we saw him coming back toward his lodge held up between two stout young Indians who had just pulled him out of a ditch — his fine coat covered with mud, his hat battered and bruised, his spear shorn of its gay streamers, and poor Pawnee himself weeping and uttering all the doleful lamentations of a tipsy Indian."],
  s28: ["One of the first women I came to know was the wife of Wau-kaun-zee-kah, the Yellow Thunder. She had gone east with her husband when he was one of the deputation that visited the President, and ever since then she had been known as the Washington woman. There was a pleasant, old-acquaintance air about the way she greeted me, as much as to say, you and I have both seen something of the world. Not one word of surprise or admiration got past her lips. Her companions exclaimed over everything I showed them and clapped their hands with a childlike, laughing simplicity, and she looked on with a deportment that said plainly, yes, yes, my children, I have seen all these things before. What broke her was a shell. I had a little cabinet of them, and I held a tropical one up to her ear, and she heard the murmur come out of it. She poked her finger into the opening to get at the animal inside. She shook it hard. She put it to her ear again. And then she burst out in a hearty laugh and set it down, admitting with her face what she would not say, that this was past her understanding.", "I had one shell of peculiar beauty, the favorite of the whole collection, a small conch covered over with rich dark veins. Every one of the visitors took that shell up in turn, and said and signed her admiration of it, plainly showing that she had an eye for beauty. This was on the occasion of the parting visit of my red daughters.", "Shortly after the payment was finished and the Indians had gone, I found that my valued shell was missing from the collection. Could one of the squaws have taken it? It was possible. They will sometimes do such things, though rarely, under a strong enough temptation. I went back over the party in my mind, trying to fix on the one who looked most likely to have been the culprit. It could not have been the Washington woman. She was partly civilized. She knew better.", "A few weeks afterward Mrs. Yellow Thunder appeared again. She was carrying a gay-colored chintz shawl rolled up in her hand, and she unfolded it carefully, and took out the shell, and laid it on the table in front of me. I could not decide whether to let my face show displeasure at the trick she had played me or joy at having my treasure back, and settled at last on the policy of showing no emotion at all.", "She stayed on until my husband came in, and he then questioned her about the matter.", "\"She had taken the shell to her village, to show to some of her people who did not come to the payment.\"", "\"Why had she not asked her mother's leave before carrying it away?\"", "\"Because she saw that her mother liked the shell, and she was afraid she would say, No.\"", "That was not the first time Madame Washington had displayed the shrewdness that was the ruling trait of her character. During the visit of the Indians to the Eastern cities they were taken to all sorts of exhibitions, museums, menageries, theatres. It did not escape their notice that a little silver always changed hands before anyone went in, and they asked why. It was explained to them. The woman brightened up like a person who has just been handed an idea.", "\"How much do you pay for each one?\"", "Her Father told her.", "\"How do you say that in English?\"", "\"Two shillings.\"", "\"Two shinnin — humph.\" Good.", "The next day, when visitors began flocking as usual to the rooms where the Indians were staying, the woman and a young Indian she had taken on as confederate posted themselves by the door and kept it shut. Whenever anyone knocked, the door opened a cautious few inches, and a hand came out of it, and the woman said, \"Two shinnin.\"", "It was readily paid every time, and the game went on until she had accumulated a considerable sum. But that did not satisfy her. At the first attempt of a visitor to leave the room, the door was held close as before, the hand was put out, and \"Two shinnin\" met his ear again. He tried to explain that having paid to come in, he must be allowed to go out free. She gave an innocent shake of the head. \"Two shinnin\" was all the English she could understand.", "The Agent had come in a little while before, and had overheard the whole of it, and had been sitting behind his newspaper laughing and waiting to see how it would end. He now came forward and interfered, and the guests were permitted to go out without a further contribution.", "The good woman was also admonished that it is far from the custom of white people to tax their friends and visitors in this manner, and that the practice must be laid aside in future.", "Another instance of the disposition of the Indians to avail themselves of every good thing fortune throws in their way was the following.", "On the same trip, while they were passing through Ohio, one of the party asked the Agent, \"Do you pay for all those provisions that are set before us at the hotels?\"", "\"Yes. Why do you ask?\"", "\"Nothing: I thought you maybe paid for just what we ate of them.\"", "At the next stopping-place a fine breakfast was set on the table, and they partook of it plentifully, as usual. Just as they had finished, the horn sounded for everyone to take their places in the stage-coaches. Every man sprang to his feet. One seized the plates of biscuits and poured them into the corner of his blanket. Another took the remains of a pair of chickens. A third emptied the sugar-bowls. Each one laid hold of whatever was nearest him, and in a trice there was nothing left on that table but empty plates and dishes. The landlord and the waiters stood watching it and laughing, enjoying the trick as much as any of the spectators.", "On another occasion their Father had tried to impress upon them the unseemliness of throwing their refuse pieces and bones and fragments of food about on the table-cloth, pointing out the orderly manner of the whites at table and the propriety of keeping everything neat and nice around them.", "At their next meal they were served first with a chicken-pie, of which they ate very heartily, so that the accumulation of bones on their plates was very abundant. Then another and more favorite dish appeared — a fine, large, roasted turkey. A gentleman sitting near it was evidently preparing to carve. There was no time to lose. But what was to be done with the bones? They looked around them in some perplexity. A large apple-pie stood close by. The most eager of them drew it toward him and, quick as thought, laid every bone on top of it; then, with a triumphant laugh at the happy idea, he coolly transferred the bird to his own dish and carried it off to distribute among his companions. The amazed stranger soon joined in the laugh at the unceremonious manner in which his share of the dinner had vanished."],
  s29: ["The payment was over. The Indians had scattered to their wintering grounds and the traders had gone too, carrying off a good share of the silver, in exchange for which each family had provided itself, so far as it could, with clothing, guns, traps, ammunition and the rest of what a winter takes. The Indians are good at a bargain. They are not easily overreached. They know an exorbitant charge the moment they hear one, and a trader who tries his shrewdness on them is certain to be given an expressive nickname that sticks to him ever after.", "M. Rolette, for instance, they called Ah-kay-zaup-ee-tah — five more — because, they said, whatever number of skins a man offered him for an article, his terms were invariably five more.", "A lady once said to him, \"Oh, M. Rolette, I wouldn't be engaged in the Indian trade, it seems to me a system of cheating the poor Indians.\"", "\"Let me tell you, madame,\" he replied, with great naïveté, \"it isn't so easy a thing to cheat the Indians as you imagine. I have tried it these twenty years, and have never succeeded!\"", "We settled down after that into a quiet, domestic life. The military system marked out the whole of it. The bugle-call at reveille, answered by a very good band; the light, animated strains for sick-call, and, shortly after, for breakfast; the longer ceremony of guard-mounting; the Old English Roast-Beef to announce the dinner-hour; the sweet, plaintive Lochaber no more at retreat, followed most incongruously by The Little Cock-Sparrow; and at last the long, rolling tattoo late in the evening. The day came to us in pieces, and with books and music and drawing on top of the household work, the time flew faster than it had ever gone before. It was Sunday when I most missed my Eastern home. I had planned out beforehand what we should do on the first return of that sacred day under our own roof. At the very least, I told myself, we shall have the Sabbath's quiet and repose; and I can, among other things, do poor Louisa some good by giving her additional lessons of a serious character.", "So while she was taking away the breakfast-things I said to her, \"Now, Louisa, get your work all finished, and everything put neatly aside, and then come here to me again.\"", "\"Yes, ma'am.\"", "We sat down to our books. We read and waited. We waited and read another hour. No Louisa.", "There was music and the sound of voices on the parade in front of our windows, but that did not disturb us. We were used to it every day of our lives.", "At last I had to go down and see what was keeping my damsel. The breakfast-things stood where she had set them on the table. The kettles and the spider were on the hearth. The fire was out. The kitchen was empty.", "Coming back through the hall, which runs the whole length of the house and opens at the front onto the parade, I saw a group collected out in the area, of all shades and colors, and in the middle of it one round woolly head I could not possibly mistake, bobbing up and down, now on this side and now on that, while peals of laughter came out of the whole group at once.", "\"Louisa,\" I called. \"Come here. What are you doing there?\"", "\"Looking at inspection.\"", "\"But why aren't your breakfast-things washed, and your kitchen swept?", "Did I not tell you I wished you to come up and learn your lessons?\"", "\"Yes, ma'am, but I had to see inspection first. Everybody looks at inspection on Sunday.\"", "I found it was useless to expect anything more of Louisa than an afternoon's lesson, and with that I had to content myself.", "It seemed to me that it would be pleasant, and perhaps profitable, if all the inmates of the garrison were to assemble on that day, and one of our number be found who would read a portion of the church-service, with a sermon out of one of our several selections.", "I approached the subject cautiously, with an inquiry to this effect:", "\"Are there none among the officers who are religiously disposed?\"", "\"Oh, yes,\" replied the one I put it to, \"there is S — —, when he is half tipsy, he takes his Bible and 'Newton's Works,' and goes to bed and cries over them, he thinks in this way he is excessively pious.\"", "S — — was among the officers who had never visited us. It seemed fair to infer that if his religious principles did not correct his own evil habits, they would not do much toward improving anybody else's, and that there was no use calling in his co-operation for any scheme for a better observance of the Lord's day.", "We had to content ourselves with writing to our friends in the East, to interest them in getting a missionary sent out to us who could officiate as chaplain in the garrison — a plan the officers seemed to favor. The hope of any united religious service was, for the present, laid aside.", "The post-surgeon got a furlough about then, and his place was filled by Dr. Newhall, of Galena; and with the addition of his gentle, quiet wife, our circle of ladies was enlarged to three. Here we were, in a wilderness. And yet how contented and happy.", "The gloom that replaced that tranquillity came to the door on its own legs. A Frenchman named Letendre presented himself one day without warning. He had come from Chicago, and he brought the distressing intelligence that our dear relative Dr. Wolcott was extremely — indeed hopelessly — ill. My husband began at once to prepare for instant departure. I begged to be allowed to go with him, but he meant to travel at a speed that made it impossible, and he refused my entreaties. Within a few hours his provisions and horses and everything else necessary for the trip were ready, and he set off with Petaille Grignon, his usual attendant on such expeditions, leaving Letendre to follow when he had recovered from his fatigue.", "The hours of his absence were sad and dreary ones, for all the kind efforts of our friends to cheer me. In a few days another messenger came in with the news of the fatal termination of Dr. Wolcott's illness. That noble heart, so full of warm and kindly affections, had ceased to beat, and sad and desolate indeed were those who had so loved and honored him.", "My husband came back as soon as he could possibly leave the family, and it was fortunate he had delayed no longer, because the winter was setting in now, and setting in with severity.", "Our quarters were spacious, but they had been built out of green trees cut down in the forest and sawed into boards by the hands of the soldiers, and green wood shrinks and warps. It left a great many yawning crevices. Stuffing the cracks with cotton batting and pasting strips of paper over them was the employment of many a leisure hour.", "Then there were the chimneys. In spite of all the currents of air moving through those rooms, which might have been expected to create a draught, the chimneys had a sad habit of smoking. To remedy it, a couple of gun-barrels were sawed off, by order of the commanding officer, and set into the hearth, one on each side of the fire-place, in the hope that air from the room below would help carry the smoke up where it belonged.", "The next morning after this had been done, Louisa was washing the hearth.", "\"Pray, ma'am,\" she said, \"what are these things put in here for?\"", "I explained what they were for.", "\"Oh, I am so glad it is only that! Uncle Ephraim (Major Twiggs's servant) said they were to be filled with powder and fired off Christmas Day, and he was terribly afraid they would blow the house up, and we in it.\"", "Ephraim was a most faithful and valuable servant, and he often amused himself by playing on the credulity of the younger portion of the colored fraternity.", "\"Is it true,\" Louisa asked me another day, \"that Pillon and Plante were once prairie-wolves?\"", "\"Prairie-wolves! what an idea! Why do you ask such a foolish question?\"", "\"Because Uncle Ephraim says they, and all the Frenchmen about here, were once prairie-wolves, and that, living so near the white people, they grow, after a time, to be like them, and learn to talk and dress like them. And then, when they get to be old, they turn back into prairie-wolves again, and that all the wolves that the officers bait with their dogs used to be Frenchmen, once.\"", "After a while I stopped correcting Uncle Ephraim's stories. I was arriving, by slow degrees, at the conviction that my little colored damsel was by no means so simple and unsophisticated as she would have me believe, and that the person being imposed upon in that house was myself.", "The snow that winter was prodigious and the cold was intense. Water would freeze in our parlors a very short distance from the fire. The fatigue-parties kept the halls filled with wood almost to the ceiling, but that could not counterbalance the inconvenience of having the wide doors standing open to the outer air a great part of the day while they carried it in. We Northerners ought to have had wood-houses built for the purpose, and ought not only to have kept our great hall-doors shut but to have protected them with a hurricane-house besides. But the Florida frontier is not a climate in which our Southern bachelors could have picked up any knowledge that would be of use when the thermometer stands at twenty-five degrees below zero — a point at which the brandy congealed in the sideboard."],
  s30: ["Christmas and New Year brought our Ho-chunk friends back to the Portage. They had learned something of the holidays from their French neighbors, and I had been warned in advance that I would see the women kissing every white man they met. I did not entirely believe that. I did believe the rest of it — that each of them would expect a present, a compliment of the season — so I laid in a supply of beads, ribbons, combs, and whatever other small bright things I had. They were fond of dainties, all of them. The day before, I had a great quantity of crullers and doughnuts fried, so there would be something ready in the house that they would actually want.", "The frying had been left to Louisa. When the cakes came up to be put away in the \"Davis,\" only a moderate share of them were there, which surprised and annoyed me.", "\"Where are the rest of the cakes, Louisa?\"", "\"That great fellow, Hancock, came in with the fatigue-party to fill the water-barrels, and while I had just stepped into the store-room to get some more flour, he carried off all I had got cooked.\"", "She made a face and whined it out, as if she had not herself treated every soldier who ever set foot on the premises.", "Early the next morning I had quite a levee of Ho-tshung-rah matrons. They came in one after another and seated themselves in a circle on the floor, and I was sorry to see that soap and water had formed no part of their holiday preparation where their blankets were concerned. There was no one to interpret. I thought I would open the talk in a language they would understand, so I took a china dish from the sideboard, filled it with the brown crullers, grated a good quantity of white sugar over them as one does, and handed it to the first woman in the circle. She took the dish out of my hand, tipped every cake into the corner of her blanket, and gave the dish back to me empty. She must be a very hungry woman, I thought. I will manage better next time. I filled the dish again and went to the second, keeping fast hold of the rim so that she could take one modestly. She scooped the whole of it out with both hands and put the cakes away in her blanket like the first. My sense of politeness rebelled at doling them out one at a time the way you do with children, so I sat down to think, because the supply would clearly not stretch to that kind of demand, and there would be more visitors before long.", "While I sat there at a loss, the ones who had the cakes began sharing them out, and the whole number was divided fairly around the circle. But they did not eat them. They ran their fingers over the grated sugar, looked into one another's faces, and muttered low. There was something here they did not understand. At last one, bolder than the rest, wet her finger, picked up a few grains, and put it carefully to her mouth.", "\"Tah-nee-zhoo-rah!\" — Sugar! — she said, delighted, and the whole room broke into a hearty laugh. The cakes went after that as fast as good breeding allowed. They had never seen any sugar but the brown or yellow maple. They had taken the white for salt, and that was why not one of them would taste it.", "They stayed on and on, until Shaw-nee-aw-kee at last made his appearance, and then, having been made happy by their various gifts, they all took their leave together."],
  s31: ["A letter reached Mr. Kinzie around this time from Colonel Richard M. Johnson, of Kentucky. The Colonel had thrown himself with great warmth behind a school got up in that State for the schooling of Indian youths and children, and he wanted something from the Agent. He wanted him to work on the Winnebagoes — to press them to send their children there, and, what mattered a good deal more to the Colonel, to press them to lay aside some share of their annuity-money each year to keep the place running.", "It was the holiday season, and a good many of the chiefs happened to be camped near the Portage. A messenger went out to gather them in at Paquette's house — Paquette was the interpreter — so that their Father could talk with them there.", "They came, all of them, on the day named. The letter was opened out before them, and everything that could be said for civilization and education was said: what it would mean for the whole nation if even a handful of the younger ones were properly taught by the whites and then sent home again to pass on to their people the learning, the arts, the manufactures, the habits of a civilized life. At the end of each point, as it was rendered to them, the answer came back from all of them together — \"Humph!\" (Good!)", "Their Father finished. Then Day-kau-ray, the oldest of the chiefs there and the one they held in most reverence, got to his feet and said this:", "\"Father, - The Great Spirit made the white man and the Indian. He didn't make them alike. He gave the white man a heart to love peace, and the arts of a quiet life. He taught him to live in towns, to build houses, to make books, to learn all things that would make him happy and prosperous in the way of life appointed him. To the Native man the Great Spirit gave a different character. He gave him a love of the woods, of a free life, of hunting and fishing, of making war with his enemies and taking scalps. The white man doesn't live like the Indian - it isn't his nature. Neither does the Indian love to live like the white man - the Great Spirit didn't make him so.", "\"Father, - We don't wish to do anything contrary to the will of the Great Spirit. If he had made us with white skins, and characters like the white men, then we would send our children to this school to be taught like the white children.", "\"Father, - We think that if the Great Spirit had wished us to be like the whites, he would have made us so. As he hasn't seen fit to do so, we believe he would be displeased with us, to try and make ourselves different from what he thought good.", "\"Father, - I have nothing more to say. This is what we think. If we change our minds, we will let you know.\"", "What Day-kau-ray says there shows what the Indians hold to be true: that the arts and the sciences come to the white man because the Great Spirit himself is his teacher in them. The Great Spirit has given the Indian no such lessons. To go after that knowledge by some side road of his own would therefore be a thing beneath him."],
  s32: ["Little incidents of that kind, and now and then a dinner or a tea-party for the young officers — sometimes at the Major's quarters, sometimes at ours — carried our life along pleasantly enough. Now and again I amused myself by making something very nice, a fruit cake or a pie, and sending it over to the young officers' quarters as a present, on the theory that a mess with no lady to preside over it might run short of that sort of thing. Mrs. Twiggs had well-trained servants to do for her what, thanks to my little dark handmaid, always came back around to me.", "One day I made some mince pies. The Major and my husband both approved of them highly, and I thought I would send one to each of the young officers.", "It happened that my husband, coming back that same day from superintending his men on the other side of the river, had an errand that took him to Captain Harney's quarters.", "Dinner had just gone on the table. The Captain would not hear of his leaving, and sat him down with himself and another gentleman who was there. The pork and beans were pronounced excellent. When they were cleared away, a mince pie came on.", "The Captain cut it and helped his guests, then took a piece himself and began to taste it. He pushed his plate back with a jerk and an exclamation, and called to his servant, a little thick-set mulatto who waited on him. \"David, you yellow rascal, how dare you put such a pie on my table?\" Then he turned to the company to apologize. \"If there is anything on earth David does understand, it is how to make a mince pie, and here he has filled this with brandy, so we can't eat a morsel of it!\"", "\"Please, sir,\" said David, modestly, \"I didn't make the pie — it is one Mrs. Kinzie sent as a present.\"", "That left the poor Captain nowhere to stand. He raved at himself in front of them all, and begged my husband in the most earnest terms not to tell me what a mistake he had made — a request that went straight out of my husband's head the moment he was inside his own door. As for the unlucky Captain, he did not venture to call on me again until he was quite sure the whole circumstance had gone out of mine."],
  s33: ["Early in January the snow came down and did not stop. We had an unusual quantity of it at the Portage. In the diggings, as the lead-mining country was called, it lay at a depth nobody had heard of before — five or six feet on the level, with the drifts on top of that.", "The officers had sent an express to Chicago to carry our letters down and bring the mail back. The messenger was a soldier named Sulky, a tough, hardy man who had always made light of the distance and of the pack on his shoulders, and who went the whole way on foot. Sulky had now been gone some weeks. We had stopped watching the road for him. We had given him up altogether, and settled it privately among ourselves that he had died somewhere out there of cold and hunger.", "Then, at last, he came in. He was nearly blind from traveling in the snow, and had to be led the final part of the way. He had lain by three weeks in an Indian lodge because the drifts were too deep to let him make a step, and the account he gave of the country between here and there finished the hopes I had been quietly building of getting down to see our friends at Chicago that winter.", "We had been laying plans for it before the last heavy fall. Captain Harney had kindly begun putting together some trains — boxes set on sledges — which lined with buffalo-skins would have made a very comfortable sort of vehicle, and I was still inclined to think that a good deep bed of snow over the whole country was no great obstacle to a sleigh-ride. The commanding officer ended the discussion. He had been violently against the scheme from the first, and he stated plainly that he would order the sentinels to fire on us if we tried to leave the fort. The majority was against us. We gave it up.", "Sweet, lovely little Lizzie Twiggs arrived before January was quite out, and there was light and joy in two houses at once. She seemed to belong to all of us. As she grew in size and beauty it was hard to say which of us was proudest of her. Whatever slow hours we might have had before, we had none now — she was the pet, the darling, the joint property of both households.", "Any regret I had felt about leaving my friend for the three weeks we meant to allow ourselves in Chicago went away with that. She would hardly miss me now. We could hold ourselves ready to move on the first break in the weather and carry out the plan at last.", "Through the last part of February the cold slackened. The snow melted off, and by the beginning of March the weather had turned so warm and genial that we were confident of making the trip on horseback without any serious trouble.", "Once the thing was settled, the first question was warm clothing. A riding-habit of stout broadcloth was pronounced indispensable to my equipment. I did not own one. Nothing in my wedding traveling gear came anywhere near answering the purpose. The cloth itself could be had in abundance at the sutler's store — the shantee, as everyone called it — but that left the question of who was to turn it into a garment.", "The regimental tailor was sent for. He was also cook to one of the companies, and there was some doubt at first whether he could be spared from the spit for the needle as long as I would need him. All his tailoring until then had been done at odd hours on a bench in the company kitchen, and he proposed to carry the riding-habit over there and work on it in the usual way. I pointed out that if I was to superintend the work I should then have to take up my abode in the barracks for the duration, which would be a decided inconvenience.", "He got around the difficulty himself, and was in the end so happy as to find a soldier in Company D who consented to officiate in his place at the kitchen fire until his term of service to me should expire.", "So picture a little solemn-looking man in his stocking-feet, sitting cross-legged on an Indian mat by my parlor window. He had arranged everything to suit himself, and I judged it wisest not to interfere. The cutting-out was the hard part, and since he had never made a lady's riding-habit in his life, the cutting-out fell to me. I was as complete a novice as he was. I will admit that this first effort of mine was open to criticism. The little tailor did not think so. He was in an ecstasy over our joint performance.", "\"Upon my word, madam,\" he would say, standing back to survey it with admiring eyes, \"we shall have a very respectable garment!\" I could not count how many times he said it in the three days the work took.", "I do not believe he had perfect confidence in the culinary powers of his comrade in Company D. Every day, half an hour before the beat of the drum, the work was folded and set aside, the snips gathered up, everything restored to order. Then he would slip out, put on the shoes he had left outside the door in the Turkish manner, and hurry across to the barrack-kitchen to see how matters stood.", "Downstairs, in the meantime, great preparations were going forward under our tidy, active little French servant, Mrs. Pillon, the wife of one of the engagés, who had taken the place of the irregular and unmanageable Louisa.", "Biscuits were baked. A ham, some tongues, and several pieces of salt pork were boiled. Coffee was roasted and ground, sugar cracked, isinglass cut up into pieces of the size wanted for a pot of coffee. Cotton bags of different sizes had been made up beforehand to hold each of these things, and large sacks of skin, which the Canadians call porches, were provided for the bulkier provisions. It was going to be a long trip.", "The distance from Fort Winnebago to Chicago is not formidable if you take the direct route. We knew the direct route was impossible at that season. The road by Kosh-ko-nong was out of the question, since all the Indians were away from their villages in the winter, and with the ice gone there would be no means of getting across the Rock River at that place.", "That left one way only: south to Dixon — Ogie's Ferry, as it was called then — the sole certain crossing of that broad, fast water. The detour ran so far out of our true course that we could not hope to do it in under six days, and we laid in our provisions accordingly.", "While the wardrobe and the food were being got ready, the other arrangements were settled — who would come with us and what we would ride.", "Mr. Kinzie decided to take only two men, Plante and Pierre Roy. Plante was to act as guide, on his own assurance that he knew every mile of the way from the Portage to Ogie's Ferry, and from Ogie's Ferry to Chicago.", "The merits of the different saddle-horses were argued over and the most eligible one picked out for my use. For a while we hesitated between Le Gris and Souris, two much-vaunted animals belonging to Paquette, the interpreter. In the end, being governed like most of my sex by a regard for exterior, I chose Le Gris. Souris was given to young Roy. My own little stumpy pony, Brunet, was pronounced just the thing for a pack-saddle. My husband rode his own bay horse, Tom, and Plante, the gayest and proudest of the party, was mounted on a fine large animal named Jerry that had lately been bought for me. With that our cortége was complete."],
  s34: ["We said our goodbyes, and on the morning of the eighth of March we were mounted and ready. It was a fine day. The streams had already gone green along their edges and were throwing the sun back at us, and everything about that morning promised a kind season, and an early one. At the ferry below the hill the fort stood on, Major Twiggs tried once more, and then again, to talk me out of going. He told me plainly that the journey would be dangerous past anything I was capable of imagining. I would not be moved.", "Every young officer in the garrison had decided he would ride out with us as far as Duck Creek, four miles off, and none of them would hear of being refused. One or two, I think, would gladly have come the whole way, if only to break the long sameness of a life in which nothing whatever occurs. Behind us came an ox cart with a canoe riding on top of it. The canoe was to carry us over the creek, and over a wide marsh beyond it that at this time of year always stood deep in water. We laughed most of the way at the cart, which pitched and banged along a road that had never before in its life been asked to consider a wheeled carriage.", "Our Canadian — a short, shock-headed, sunburnt, thick-lipped man who by some miracle happened to be the husband of my pretty servant, Mrs. Pillon — kept up a running roar at the oxen whenever they slackened or knocked the cart against a stump. \"Marchez, don-g,\" \"regardez,\" \"prenez garde,\" and we found it endlessly funny. My own spirits were high. I could see no hardship anywhere ahead of me and had somehow got it into my head that I was setting out on a pleasure excursion across the prairies. That a straw bonnet and kid gloves might be the wrong things to wear had not once occurred to me. I had never travelled in so raw a season and knew nothing at all about preparing for one; when my husband suggested blanket socks and a woollen capuchon for my head and shoulders, I laughed at him and went without. So when the wind got under my head-gear and shoved it about with a rude puff, and when my hands began to swell and stiffen with the cold, I told myself these were trifles and that I would soon be used to them. I was far too pleased with the novelty of my outfit to mind — the hunting-knife in its gay scabbard hanging at my neck, the tin cup at my saddle-bow.", "At Duck Creek we parted from the young men, who stayed on the bank long enough to watch us over: ourselves in the canoe, the poor horses swimming, the water carrying cakes of ice.", "The far bank rose, and past the rise lay the marsh, perhaps three hundred yards of it. The men hauled the canoe across to its edge. The water there was not deep, so our people only lifted the pack off Brunet and my side-saddle off Le Gris, so that nothing would be lost if anything went wrong, and then got on their own horses and led the two spare ones. My husband set the pack-horse's load and my saddle in the middle of the canoe and took up the paddle.", "\"Now, wifie,\" he said, \"jump in, and seat yourself flat in the bottom of the canoe.\"", "\"Oh, no,\" I said, \"I will sit on the little trunk in the center. I shall be so much more comfortable, and I can balance the canoe exactly.\"", "\"As you please, but I think you will find it isn't the best way.\"", "He shoved off hard and we slid a few feet clear of the bank. The two greyhounds we had brought with us had been standing there whining, refusing the water they had been ordered into. Now they made up their minds. They jumped together and came down square on top of me. For a moment the canoe held. Then it gave, and quick as that, dogs and baggage and lady went into the deepest water there was.", "My husband had been on the point of stepping in himself when the dogs so unceremoniously went ahead of him. He was beside me before I had time to be afraid, and he had me by the collar of my cloak, begging me not to be frightened. I was not, in the least. I was laughing while he lifted me out and set me back on the bank.", "The saddle and the little trunk were fished out after me, thoroughly soaked. And then our amusement was topped altogether by the maladroit Pillon, who was making a hopeless business of riding my spirited Jerry across the marsh. He had both arms around the animal's neck and a face wrung out of shape with terror, and he was shouting every French objurgation he owned. Jerry pranced and curveted, and then pitched his rider — his burden, rather — headforemost, several feet out into the water.", "A general howl of mirth went up at the unfortunate Frenchman, and doubled when he came up out of his watery bed puffing and snorting and waddled back to the place he had started from. The horse, meanwhile, showed the better sense of the two and made his way off to join his companions, who had already reached the farther bank.", "\"Well, wifie,\" said Mr. Kinzie, \"I can't trust you in the canoe again. There is no way but to carry you across the marsh like a pappoose. Will you take a ride on my shoulders?\"", "\"With all my heart, if you will promise to take me safely.\" And I was soon mounted.", "I must confess that the gentleman staggered once or twice under me, for I was no light load, and more than once I was quite sure I was about to go the way of old Pillon. But we reached the other side in safety.", "There he made me change into dry shoes and stockings and — must I admit it? — drink a little brandy against the effects of my icy bath. He wanted to stop altogether, kindle a fire and dry my clothing and my wardrobe properly, and I would not hear of it. I argued that standing still would chill me worse than riding on in a wet habit and cloak, and that may well have been true. But there was something else mixed in with my convictions, which was that I could not bear to give our friends at the fort the pleasure — and they would certainly have taken it — of laughing at so inauspicious a commencement.", "The horses were soon put in order and we started again. It was a fine day for the season. The wet clothes never troubled me at all; the exercise of riding took the chill straight out. The whole manner of travelling was new to me, and I liked it the better for having been shut up more than five months inside the walls of the fort, where the only variation on any day was an occasional walk of half a mile into the surrounding woods.", "We lost a little more time on the road when we met Lapierre, the blacksmith from Sugar Creek, who was going up to the Portage for supplies with one of his associates. So we had come no more than twenty-three miles when we reached the ground we meant to camp on. It lay along a beautiful stream that feeds one of the Four Lakes — that chain whose banks have no equal anywhere for romantic loveliness.", "I could only admire the sagacity of the horses. They seemed to know, the way a person knows, that they were coming to the place where the day's toil would end. Long before we were near the point of woods that meant a halt, their ears went up and their pace quickened, and they came into the spot at a full gallop.", "We got down in an open space just inside the verge of the wood — the timber, as travelers in the West call it. My husband told me to walk about and keep moving until there was a fire, and there was one soon enough; our woodsmen were active and practised, and a large tree came down under them in a very few minutes. The dry grass all around made excellent tinder, and the sparks off the flint took it — there were no loco-focos in those days — and with broken branches and bits of light-wood fed in, we had a cheering flame. Meanwhile the bourgeois put up the tent, facing the fire, but set so that the wind would carry the smoke and flame away from the opening instead of into it. Inside, on the ground, went a bear-skin, then two or three blankets — each rider carried two, one under the saddle and one above it — and once the rest of the luggage had been brought in I could get out of every wet thing I had on and into dry ones. If you want to know what the thermometer was doing, consider my riding-habit. It was laid over the end of the huge log our fire was built against, and within a very few minutes it had frozen so stiff that it stood upright on its own, a dress out of which a lady had vanished by some means nobody could account for.", "To describe the ham broiled on the \"broches,\" the toasted bread, the steaming coffee and the primitive table-furniture would only be to tell the Fox River over again. There is this difference. On horseback we carry a coffee-pot, a tea-kettle, and for each rider a tin cup and a hunting-knife, and nothing else. Ceremony at table does not exist. A man draws his knife out of the scabbard, and if he happens to think of it he gives it a wipe on the napkin. Its first duty is to stir the cup of coffee. Its second is to divide the piece of ham lying on the half of a traveling biscuit held in the left hand, the biscuit doing the office of a plate. To cut the meat cleanly without also destroying the dish beneath it is an art that comes only with long practice.", "We take our places around the mat and eat, and after a ride like that day's we find it delicious. The Frenchmen sit a little distance off with their own supplies of coffee and meat and bread, and now and then pass a joke with the bourgeois, who is a demi-god to them; nothing he does, however affable, however condescending, ever costs him an ounce of their respect or their devotion.", "When the meal is finished, the table-furniture is rinsed out in hot water and set aside until morning. A wisp of dry prairie-grass is generally judged enough to make a knife fit to go back into its scabbard. At this season there is no amusement to be had but watching the spancelled horses shuffle awkwardly from one spot to the next in search of pasturage, so it is not long before we arrange our blankets and lie down to rest.", "At break of day the bourgeois shouts us awake — \"How! how! how!\"", "Everyone is up at once. The fire, fed once or twice through the night, is brought back to a flame. The horses are caught and saddled while a breakfast, the same food as the evening before, is got ready; the tent comes down; the pack-horse is loaded; \"tout démanché,\" as the Canadian says. We eat, rinse the kettles and the cups and tie them to our saddle-bows, and then we are mounted and away, leaving our fire, or rather our smoke, to tell of our visit."],
  s35: ["March 9th. — That day's ride took us past the first of the Four Lakes. There was an encampment of Winnebagoes scattered along the bank, and they greeted their Father with vociferous joy. \"Bon-jour, bon-jour, Shaw-nee-aw-kee!\" \"Hee-nee-kar-ray-kay-noo?\" How do you do?", "Then came the usual announcement. \"Wys-kap-rah tshoonsh-koo-nee-noh!\" I have no bread.", "That is their form of begging. We could not afford to be generous. There was no certainty of getting any more if our own gave out, and that obliged us to the strictest economy.", "How beautiful the encampment looked in the morning sun. The matted lodges with the blue smoke curling off their tops, the trees and bushes powdered with the light snow that had come down in the night, the lake shining and sparkling almost at our feet — even the Indians in their own costume added to the picture.", "I was sorry to leave it, and sorrier to leave it in a hurry, but Souris, the pack-horse, had taken it into his head to decamp while we stood talking with our red friends. He had at least been sensible enough to set off in the right direction, so we had the good fortune to overtake him after a short race. Young Roy, who had particular charge of him, gave him a great deal of scolding and some blows, and then he was put in the middle of the cavalcade as a mark of disgrace for his breach of duty.", "After the lake our road ran over rolling prairie, bare and desolate enough now. The hollows were full of snow, and the snow was half thawed, so the footing was never certain, and I could not help joining in the ringing laughter of our Frenchmen when Brunet and Souris, the two ponies, went floundering through the yielding stuff almost up to their bodies. Even the vainglorious Plante, who piqued himself on his horsemanship, was nearly unhorsed once or twice, having chosen his road badly. Now and then the higher ground carried a thicket or a copse, and our dogs would put up a deer or two out of it. The first bound, the first long lope, was the signal for a chase. The horses seemed to catch the spirit of the thing as halloo answered halloo, but we were never fortunate enough to get a shot at one, and though the dogs came up with a deer once or twice they were not strong enough to hold it. It was about the middle of the afternoon when we reached the Blue Mound. I was very glad to have got that far, because I was sadly fatigued by then and every mile now felt like two to me. The miles are unconscionably long in that country. When I was told we still had seven of them to go to Morrison's, where we meant to stop for the night, I was almost in despair. It was my first trip on horseback and I was not yet inured to the exercise.", "When we came to Morrison's I was so far spent that as my husband reached up to lift me from the saddle, I fell into his arms.", "\"This will never do,\" he said. \"Tomorrow we must turn our faces toward Fort Winnebago again.\"", "The door opened hospitably to take us in. We were welcomed by a lady with a most sweet, benignant face, and by a companion some years younger. The first was Mrs. Morrison. The other was Miss Elizabeth Dodge, daughter of General Dodge.", "My husband laid me on a small bed in the room where the two ladies had been sitting at their work. They took off my bonnet and my riding-dress, chafed my hands, and made me some warm wine and water, and I soon came round. Half an hour of lying still refreshed me so much that I was able to talk with them and to relieve my husband's mind of all his anxiety on my account. Tea was announced soon after, and we went across to an adjoining building — Morrison's, like every settler's establishment of that period, was a group of detached log houses, each with one room in it, or at the most two.", "The table groaned with good cheer, and put me in mind of tables I had seen among the old-fashioned Dutch families on the banks of the Hudson.", "My spirits had come back and we made a cheerful party. Mrs. Morrison told us that in her first eighteen months in this country she did not speak with one white woman. Her husband and two black servant-women were the only society she had.", "A Tennessee woman had called in with her little boy just before tea, and we amused Mr. Kinzie afterward with a description of the pair. The mother's visit was simply one of courtesy. She was a little dumpy woman, her complexion burned perfectly red by the sun, her hair an exact tow-color, braided up from the forehead in front and from the neck behind, the two tails meeting on the crown of her head and fastened there with a small tin comb. Her dress was checkered homespun and a very tight fit, and as she wore no ruff and no handkerchief at her throat, she looked as if she had just been made ready for execution. She was plainly awestruck at finding visitors in the house and looked ready to leave the moment she got in. The boy was not so easily intimidated. He would not take her signs or her pinches at all, until he had sidled up to Mrs. Morrison and, pulling his old hat down farther over his eyes, begged for a whang — meaning a narrow strip of deer-skin. The lady very obligingly cut him one from a large smoked skin that she brought out of its receptacle, and mother and son took their leave, smiling, and still looking rather scared.", "After tea we went back to Mrs. Morrison's parlor, where she kindly insisted that I lie down again on the little bed and recruit myself, as she put it, for the next day's ride. My husband went out in the meantime to see his men and the horses settled.", "In the talk that followed I learned that Mrs. Morrison had spent a good deal of time in the neighborhood of my own recent home in Oneida County, that a number of the friends I had loved and valued were her friends too, and that when she heard of my arrival at Fort Winnebago she had thought of coming up there to call on me — to begin an acquaintance which had now been brought about by other and quite unexpected means.", "We talked long and pleasantly, and sat up late over it, going back through old friends and by-gone events with great satisfaction on both sides, much to the edification of Miss Dodge, and of the gentlemen when they came in and joined us again."],
  s36: ["The next morning we had a cheerful breakfast, joined by the Rev. Mr. Kent, of Galena, and then made ready to go on. I had reconciled my husband to keeping our route toward Chicago by assuring him that I felt as fresh and as bright as I had the morning we left home.", "There was some apprehension, though, about the next stage of it. We might have difficulty striking the trail to Hamilton's diggings, which was our next point.", "The directions we were given were certainly obscure. We were to follow a given trail for a certain number of miles until we came to a crossing; we were to turn into that, bearing easterly; and after a time that would bring us to a deep trail running straight to Hamilton's. In that open country there are no landmarks. One elevation is so exactly like the next that if you lose your trail there is about as much hope of regaining it as of finding a pathway in the middle of the ocean. And a trail out there is not a broad highway. It is a narrow path, cut deep by the hoofs of the horses the Indians ride in single file, and sunk so far into the sod that covers the prairie that it can be hard to make it out at a distance of a few rods.", "This was new ground to Mr. Kinzie, whose journeys from the Portage to Chicago had always been made by the direct route through Kosh-ko-nong. He made Mr. Morrison repeat the directions again and again, though Plante, our guide, swaggered and talked big all the while, and declared that he knew every hill and stream and point of woods from that spot to Chicago.", "We had not gone many miles before we discovered that Monsieur Plante was profoundly ignorant of the country. Mr. Kinzie had to take the lead himself and make his way as best he could by the directions he had been given. Nothing that looked like the promised cross trails came into view. The path we had started on kept bending away from what we knew to be the right direction, and bent further, until at last we had no choice but to abandon it altogether.", "We traveled the livelong day, halting at noon only long enough to bait the horses and eat a little ourselves. It was as gloomy and desolate a ride as I can well imagine. Rolling prairie, unbroken by forest or stream. Hillock rising after hillock, and at the top of each one we looked for a distant fringe of timber and did not find it. The same cheerless, unbounded prospect met the eye every way we turned, varied here and there only by oblong pits in the ground, like gigantic graves, where somebody had dug for signs of a lead-mine and found none.", "We were so anxious to recover our trail, and the weather was growing so cold and the wind so sharp and piercing, that we did not once turn aside from our course, not even when a gaunt prairie-wolf showed itself, as one did more than once, standing on the nearest rising ground and looking over at us as though it dared us to come. The Frenchmen would give an instinctive shout and spur their horses, and the hounds, Kelda and Cora, would rush off to the chase, but the bourgeois soon called them all back with a warning that we must attend strictly to the business of our journey. Just before sunset we crossed a muddy stream, with some difficulty, and found on the far side a scanty belt of trees that would serve as an encamping-ground. We took it gladly, having no idea whether we were near the place we were looking for or a great way off.", "We had ridden at least fifty miles since leaving Morrison's, and I was conscious of very little fatigue. What I did feel was a vague discomfort at the idea of being lost in that wild, cold region, a thing altogether different from anything I had experienced before. Then my husband's voice came, in the tone he always used with me. \"Cheer up, wifie — we will find the trail tomorrow.\" And the uneasiness went.", "The men soon had the camp comfortable, in spite of the trouble of driving tent-pins into frozen ground and the want of trees big enough to make a rousing fire. The place was a stony side-hill, as they would say in New England, where such things abound. We were not disposed to be fastidious. We ate our salt ham and toasted our bread and lent a pleased ear to the chatter of our Frenchmen, who could not sufficiently admire the heroism of Madame John amid the vicissitudes that had befallen her.", "The wind was high enough to be uncomfortable at bed-time, and it rose through the night. It snowed heavily. We lay there expecting every moment that the tent would be carried off the ground. The matter settled itself differently. The poles snapped, and the whole of it came down on us in one mass, with all the weight of snow on top.", "Mr. Kinzie roused his men and went out at their head into the neighboring wood to cut a new set of poles, leaving me to bear the burden of the whole thing on my shoulders, since my only shelter from the storm was to stay snugly housed underneath the canvas where I was.", "With some difficulty a sort of support was rigged up at last for the tent-covering, which answered our purpose tolerably well until daybreak, when our damp and miserable condition made us very glad to get up and hang about the fire until breakfast had been dispatched and the horses were saddled again.", "The prospect was not encouraging. Around us was an unbroken sheet of snow. We had no compass, and the air was so thick with driving sleet that it was often impossible to tell which way the sun was. I tied my husband's silk pocket-handkerchief over my veil to keep the wind and the icy particles off my face — they cut like a razor — but though I was shielded every way that circumstances allowed, I suffered intensely from the cold.", "We went on, mile after mile, turning into every point of woods in the hope of finding at least an Indian wigwam where we could ask our way. Every one of them was solitary and deserted. Not even the trace of a recent fire, to give us hope that there were human beings within miles of us.", "Then a shout from the foremost of the party made every heart in it leap.", "\"Une clôture! une clôture!\" A fence! A fence!", "It was almost like life coming back to the dead.", "We spurred on, and there they were — a few straggling rails crowning a rising ground at no great distance.", "And never did music sound so sweet as the crowing of a cock, which at that moment saluted our ears."],
  s37: ["We followed the fence down the far side of the slope, and in the hollow at the bottom of it stood a group of log cabins. Low, shabby, nothing about them that promised anything. The snow was coming off the ridge in sheets by then and driving flat into our faces, and I have never in my life been so glad to see anything so ugly.", "\"Whose cabins are these?\" Mr. Kinzie asked a man who was cutting wood at one of the doors.", "\"Hamilton's,\" the man said. He set the axe down in the same motion and came forward to help us dismount, because in that country hospitality is not a decision anybody makes. It is simply what happens.", "They showed us into the best-looking of the buildings. A large fire was burning in the clay chimney and the room held a genuine warmth, though I could see daylight in the gaps beside the door and the windows — gaps many inches across. A woman rose from a seat beside a sort of bread-trough that had been pressed into service as a cradle. A fine, fat baby lay in it. She wore a tidy calico dress and a shabby black silk cap trimmed with lace shabbier than the cap. She made room for us at the fire. She did not offer to take my wrappings and defences, which were heavy with snow by then and beginning to drip on her floor. Whether she was too timid to touch them or simply did not know it was expected, I could not tell.", "With my husband's help I contrived at last to get out of them. When he had seen me comfortably disposed of, and in a fair way to thaw after that freezing ride, he left me and went out to see to his men and his horses.", "He was a long time gone. I expected him back with our host. Instead he came back alone and laughing. Mr. Hamilton, he said, hesitated to present himself before me: he was unwilling that a woman who had known his family in the East should see him in his present mode of life. The feeling appeared to wear off. Before dinner he came in and was introduced to me, and was as agreeable and as polite as the son of Alexander Hamilton would naturally be.", "The housekeeper, who was the wife of one of the miners, prepared us a plain, comfortable dinner. A table was set out as long as the dimensions of the cabin would admit. The end nearest the fire had somewhat nicer furniture on it, and more delicate fare.", "Then somebody blew a horn. Ten or twelve miners came in and took their places below us at the table. They were the roughest-looking set of men I have ever beheld, and their language was as uncouth as their people. Hunting-shirts, trousers and moccasins, all of deer-skin, the shirts fringed with more of the same along the seams. Each of them wore a colored belt around the waist, and in each belt was stuck a large hunting-knife. Sitting in a row along that table they had the look of a company of brigands who had come in out of the weather.", "They called Mr. Hamilton \"Uncle Billy,\" every one of them, uniformly, though he was so much their superior. I could not help fancying that the familiarity was something we all had to pay them, and that what it bought was their good humor. The feeling was confirmed for me after the meal, when one of the company lingered behind while the rest of the gang took their departure. He had learned that we came from Fort Winnebago. He informed us that he was a discharged soldier and would like to make some inquiries about his old station and his comrades, and then he seated himself unceremoniously, without waiting to be asked, and began questioning us.", "There was such bitterness in the way he spoke of his former officers that I felt quite sure he was a deserter, and I rather suspected he had made his escape from the service because of some punishment. His face fairly distorted when he came to Captain H., to whose company he had belonged. \"There is a man in the mines,\" he said, \"who has been in his hands, and if he ever gets a chance to come within shot of him, I guess the captain will remember it. He knows well enough he darsn't set his foot in the diggings. And there's T. isn't much better. Everybody thought it a great pity that fellow's gun snapped when he so nearly had him at Green Bay.\"", "Having delivered himself of these sentiments, he marched out, to my great relief.", "Mr. Hamilton passed most of the afternoon with us. The storm raged so hard outside that going on with our trip was out of the question, and there was nothing else to do. He gave us pleasant anecdotes and reminiscences of his early life in New York, and of his adventures since he had come out to the Western wilderness. When he was obliged to leave us for a while, he furnished us with books to entertain us. The most interesting of them was the biography of his father.", "Could that illustrious man have foreseen the scene in which his life was one day to be perused — his son's home, a mining cabin banked in snow, a knife in every belt at the table — what would his sensations have been?", "The most amusing part of our experience was still ahead of us. As the evening came on I had been quietly speculating on our prospects for the night's accommodation. Our pale, melancholy-looking landlady and her fat baby were evidently the only specimens of the feminine gender about the establishment, so it was hardly reasonable to suppose that any of the other cabins contained wherewithal to furnish us a comfortable lodging. And the one we were in offered nothing of the sort to view, only two beds, uncurtained, extended against the farther wall. My doubts were resolved after a time by watching the hostess stretch a cord between the two and hang some petticoats and extra garments along it, by way of a partition. Then she invited us to occupy one of them.", "My only preparation was to wrap my cloak around me and lie down with my face to the wall. The good people were less ceremonious. At a distance of scarcely two feet, we could not be mistaken in the sound of their garments being, not laid aside, but whipped over the partition-wall between us.", "Our waking thoughts, though, were only thankfulness for so comfortable a lodging after the trials and fatigues we had undergone, and even those were of short duration. Our eyes were soon closed in slumber."],
  s38: ["Morning came up clear, with a hard bright edge on it, and the sky held nothing we needed to fear. Sleep had put us back together. We spoke of the road ahead as though the worst of it were behind us and nothing more could go wrong, which is the kind of thing people say out loud when they have just stopped being frightened. Mr. Hamilton offered to ride with us as far as his next neighbor's — twenty-five miles, a distance he waved off as trifling. Beyond that, from Kellogg's on to Ogie's Ferry on the Rock River, the track was traveled enough that we could not lose it. That was his assurance, and we took it.", "One of the miners came to see us off — the one who owned the wife and the baby, and who was, for that reason, a shade more human than the men he worked beside. His farewell was that he wished us well out of the country, and that we might never have occasion to return to it.", "\"I pity a body,\" he said, \"when I see them making such an awful mistake as to come out this way, for comfort never touched this Western country.\"", "Mr. Hamilton was as good a companion that day as he had been the day before, and a perfectly desperate rider. He set a pace that would have left me somewhere behind him in the timber if I had still been on my pony. I had the fine, noble Jerry under me now, and I needed him.", "We were all well mounted and he still nearly rode away from us. The prairie had given out. We were among the branches of the Pickatonick, in country that had gone rough and broken, and we went down into ravines and up out of them and through passes so narrow that the branches met overhead. They were interwoven and they projected, and as I came through behind the others I paid them my veil in pieces. Once they took my hat, and my head with it, and I would have hung there like Absalom if my husband had not sprung to me — Jerry being far too keen on holding his place in the race to do anything but what the mule did under the unfortunate prince.", "Nobody called a halt. We rode at that same rate until three o'clock in the afternoon, so that when we came in at last to Kellogg's there was no arguing the distance: we had made thirty miles at least. What wore on me hardest through all of it was not the pace but the little beast Brunet. He had been a saddle-horse until now, and he had been used to a place well up in the file, near the guide, near the front. Being demoted to pack-horse and put at the back did not suit him. Every time we came down to a stream the file would break apart, each rider choosing his own place to ford, and every time — every single time — the moment I turned Jerry's head into the water, Brunet came past me at a rush and flung himself into the exact spot where we meant to go. He plunged and snorted and threw the water over me, and more than once he startled Jerry sideways and very nearly put me into the stream. I learned after the first two crossings to hang back and let him have it his own way. That changed nothing. What he wanted was not to go first; it was to go in at the same instant Jerry did. In the end I struck a bargain with young Roy, who got down at every stream and held him until I was safely on the other bank.", "Kellogg's was a comfortable mansion set just inside the verge of a pleasant grove of timber, which is what a Western traveler calls a small forest. Mrs. Kellogg proved to be a very respectable-looking matron, and she let us know early that she came from the city of New York. Mr. Hamilton's presence at her table plainly gratified her, and she found occasion to tell us why: in earlier days she had done needle-work for his family.", "She gave us an excellent dinner. Afterward she settled me into a rocking-chair beside a large fire and put the \"Life of Mrs. Fletcher\" into my hands to keep me occupied, while the gentlemen went out to look over the place, and Mr. Kellogg's stock, and then, more carefully, their own animals. We had meant to push on to Dixon's before dark. The snow started again, and that settled it.", "When it came out that we were bound for Chicago, Mr. Kellogg made up his mind on the spot to come with us; he had business to do there, he said. Mrs. Kellogg set about getting him ready to leave in the morning. I argued against it. The next day was Sunday, and I do not travel on Sunday, and I pleaded for one more day where we were. I lost. There was the necessity of the case, and the weather that could not be relied on, and the plain importance of getting through this wild country as fast as we were able. All I carried was a delay in the hour of starting — late enough in the afternoon to leave us just time for the sixteen miles to Dixon's before the sun went.", "Mr. Kellogg needed almost no time to prepare. He would take two days' provisions and no more. At his brother-in-law Dixon's we would have our supper and our breakfast, and from Dixon's to Chicago was a day and a half; he knew that road well.", "By that arithmetic what remained in our own packs was enough to see us to the end of the journey. My husband asked Mrs. Kellogg to bake us another bag of biscuits anyway, against accidents, and he suggested to Mr. Kellogg that he might do well to carry something beyond his limited allowance. The good man would not have it. He was unwilling to burden his horse any further than was absolutely necessary, and at this season, he pointed out, the animals had to carry their own fodder on top of everything else. It will be seen that we had reason to rejoice in our own foresight.", "After the night before, my standards had come down considerably. So when I was shown our sleeping-apartment and understood that we would be sharing it with six men, travelers like ourselves, the only thing I felt was gratitude that every bed in the room had a full suit of blue checked curtains, which made a very tolerable substitute for a dressing-room."],
  s39: ["It was late the following day — March 13th — when we took leave of our kind hostess. She loaded us with cakes, with good wishes, and with messages for her sister Dixon and the children. We traveled pleasantly along through a country that was beautiful in spite of its wintry appearance.", "There was a house at Buffalo Grove where we stopped for half an hour. A nice-looking young girl there presented us with some maple-sugar of her own making, and entertained us with the history of a contest between two rival claimants for the patronage of the stage-wagon. Its proprietors had not yet decided whether to send it by Buffalo Grove or by another route, which she pointed out to us at no great distance. The driver, she took care to inform us, was in favor of the former. We asked whether he was a young man. She answered in the affirmative, and the blush that came with the answer explained the whole matter satisfactorily.", "Just at sunset we came down upon the dark, rapid waters of the Rock River. The ferry — the ferry we had traveled so far out of our way to take advantage of — proved to be merely a small boat, a skiff. The larger one had been swept off into the stream and carried down when the ice broke up the week previous. The water went by fast and black under the last of the light.", "My husband's first care was to get me across. He put me in the boat with the saddles and the packs, and because it was so late an hour and no time was to be lost, he ventured at the same time to hold the bridles of the two most docile horses, to guide them in swimming the river.", "When we had proceeded a few rods from the shore we were startled by a loud puffing and blowing close beside us. We looked around. To our great surprise, little Brunet was just upon our weather-bow. Determined not to be outdone by his model, Jerry, he had taken to the water on his own responsibility, and he reached the opposite shore as soon as any of the party.", "All being safely landed, a short walk brought us to the house of Mr. Dixon. He had come into the country so recently, and yet he had managed to make everything comfortable around him, and when he ushered us into Mrs. Dixon's sitting-room and seated us by a glowing wood fire, while Mrs. Dixon busied herself preparing us a nice supper, I felt that on such a trip the comfort overbalances the inconvenience.", "Mrs. Dixon was surrounded by several children. One of them leaned against the chimney-piece in the full Indian costume — calico shirt, blanket, leggings. He had a dark complexion and full, melancholy eyes, and he kept those eyes fixed on the ashes, where he was making marks with a stick. He rarely raised them to gaze at us, as children are wont to do. He interested me exceedingly, and I asked an intelligent little girl, evidently a daughter of our host, \"Who is that boy?\"", "\"Oh, that is John Ogie,\" answered she.", "\"What is the matter with him? he looks very sad.\"", "\"Oh, he is fretting after his mother.\"", "\"Is she dead, then?\"", "\"Some say she is dead, and some say she is gone away. I guess she is dead, and buried up in one of those graves yonder\" — pointing through the window to a rising ground opposite, where two or three little picketed inclosures stood.", "I felt a strong sympathy with the child, and it was increased when I asked whether he had a father. \"Oh, yes,\" the little spokeswoman replied, \"but he goes away, and drinks, and don't care for his children.\"", "\"And what becomes of John then?\"", "\"He stays here with us, and we teach him to read, and he learns dreadful fast.\"", "When the boy at last turned his large dark eyes upon me, it went to my heart. It was such a motherless look. And it was explained when, long afterward, I learned his further history. His mother was still living. He knew that she was living, and with the reserve peculiar to his people he never spoke of her to his young companions. Unable to endure the continued ill treatment of her husband, a surly, intemperate Canadian, she had left him and returned to her own family among the Pottowattamies. Years after, this boy and a brother who had also been left behind with their father found their way to the Upper Missouri to join her — she had been taken there by the Government, with the rest of her tribe, from the shores of Lake Michigan.", "A most savory supper of ducks and venison, with their accompaniments, soon smoked upon the board, and we did ample justice to it. Traveling is a great sharpener of the appetite, and so is cheerfulness, and ours had been raised by the encouraging account Mr. Dixon gave us of the rest of the route yet before us.", "\"There is no difficulty,\" he said, \"if you keep a little to the north, and strike the great Sauk trail. If you get too far to the south, you will come upon the Winnebago Swamp, and, once in that, there is no telling when you will ever get out again. As for the distance, it is nothing at all to speak of. Two young men came out here from Chicago, on foot, last fall. They got here the evening of the second day, and, even with a lady in your party, you could go on horseback in less time than that. The only thing is to be sure and get on the great track that the Sauks have made, in going every year from the Mississippi to Canada, to receive their presents from the British Indian Agent.\""],
  s40: ["The next morning came up bright and lovely, which for that season of the year was a gift, and we said our good-byes to Mr. and Mrs. Dixon in high spirits. For the first few miles our way lay along the banks of the Rock River, which rise and fall in long easy swells, and we held always eastward, on the beaten path — road, rather — that runs down to Fort Clark, or Peoria. The Sauk trail, we had been told, cut across this road about six miles out.", "When we had covered, by our own reckoning, the whole of that distance, a trail opened in front of us bearing northeast, and we halted to argue whether this was the one we wanted.", "My husband thought not. It leaned too far north for his liking, and it was faint — far fainter than any path could be that was trodden every year by so great a body of Indians on their journeys.", "Plante had no doubt whatever. This was the exact place where he and \"Piché\" had come out upon the great road the year before, on their way to Port Winnebago. \"On that very rising-ground at the point of woods, he remembered perfectly well stopping to shoot ducks, which they ate for their supper.\"", "Mr. Kellogg would commit himself to nothing. He agreed with whoever had spoken last.", "Plante was \"the guide,\" and so sure of himself that it was settled we should follow him — though not without grumbling from the bourgeois, who kept calling a halt to reopen the whole question.", "\"Now, Plante,\" he would say, \"I am sure you are leading us too far north. Why, man, if we keep on in this direction, following the course of the river, we shall bring up at Kosh-ko-nong, instead of Chicago.\"", "\"Ah! mon bourgeois,\" the Canadian would answer, light as ever, \"would I tell you this is the road if I weren't quite certain? Only one year ago I traveled it, and can I forget so soon? Oh, no - I remember every foot of it.\"", "What convinced Monsieur Plante at last was the water swinging away ahead of us in the river's great bend, and the bold rocky bluffs standing over it.", "\"Are you satisfied now, Plante?\" my husband asked him. \"By your leave, I will now play pilot myself.\" He quit the trail there and set our course as nearly due east as he could hold it.", "The weather had turned by then, and turned hard; the cold was intense. Even supposing we were right at last, the days we had thrown away were no small matter. We had taken on nothing more in the way of provisions at Dixon's, choosing to load what forage we could carry for the horses instead, since the picking around our camping-grounds was thin and never made them a proper meal. Still, the belief that we had found the right path at last carried us along, and we rode until dark, when we made a comfortable \"encampment\" at the edge of a grove beside a little stream.", "I have never known cold like that night. Salted provisions were what I was used to, and they left me with a thirst past bearing, so my husband had got into the way of setting the little tin coffee-pot full of water at the head of my bed when we lay down. That night it was a solid block of ice long before midnight. Inside the tent, wrapped up as we were in blankets, we took no harm at all. Outside the tent the air was pitiless.", "March 15th. — The bourgeois had us up and packing at the first grey show of light. The Sauk trail had to be found today, whatever it cost us. And if it were not found — what then? No one asked it aloud. No one had an answer to give.", "A little way from camp the ground softened under the horses and we were into marsh country. Wild geese, brant and ducks went up out of it screaming in their thousands, and the farther lakes and ponds lay black with them. The night's cold had frozen the shallow water we were trying to cross, but not enough: the ice would not carry a horse, and it cut their feet and ankles at every step as they broke through. Now and then we could not get forward at all and had to turn back and work our way round the head of the marsh — so that on top of everything else we carried the knowledge that we were laboring all day long and going nearly nowhere.", "We were clear of that swampy country at last, and onto firmer footing, mostly open prairie. Then the sky came down on us. The weather moderated, and with it came a snow-storm that blinded us. We had no trail we could trust and no compass; the sun was the one guide left to us, and now the air had thickened until not a man among us could have said which quarter of the heavens he stood in.", "We kept moving, and our track must have wandered badly. Many miles on we crossed an Indian trail cut deep into the ground, running square across the line we had been holding. The snow gave out, the cloud thinned enough to show us where the sun was, and we saw that this new trail ran north and south. Now what? Hold to the east, or take the path, on the chance it would carry us into the road we had spent so many days hunting? And if we took it, which way — up or down? My husband wanted south. By his reckoning we were still too far north, somewhere about the Grand Marais, or Kish-wau-kee. Mr. Kellogg and Plante both wanted north, Plante insisting that his bourgeois had overshot to the south already, that we must by now be somewhere near the Illinois River. Outvoted, my husband gave way, and we turned the horses' heads north with his whole will against it. A few miles of that was all he could stand. \"You may go north, if you please,\" he said, \"but I am convinced that the other course is right, and I shall face about - follow who will.\"", "So we came about and rode south, and the miles were long and heavy, and the only thing that broke them was poor Mr. Kellogg's fretting. \"I am really afraid we are wrong, Mr. Kinzie. I feel pretty sure that the young man is right. It looks most natural to me that we should take a northerly course, and not be stretching away so far to the south.\"", "My husband gave no sign of hearing any of it. The Frenchmen said nothing. They would have parted with their right hands sooner than set themselves against the bourgeois once he had spoken his decision, and it would never have entered their heads to volunteer an opinion or a remark unless they were asked for one.", "For mile after mile the road ran on through prairie, and then it bent round a point of woods and put us, without any warning, at the edge of an Indian village. The whole party gave a shout. Nothing answered it. We galloped up among the wigwams and not a voice called back to us, not a dog barked. The place was as still as a grave. We rode round it and round it, then got down and put our heads into several of the big huts. They had stood empty a long while. Bare bark walls, and nothing else — every article of furniture stripped out and carried off by the owners to their wintering-grounds, to be brought back in the spring when they came again to lay out their corn-fields and take up their summer cabins.", "What that did to us I will not try to set down. We got back into the saddle heavy-hearted and went on, and the snow began again, and made a bad situation worse. In time we halted for the night. We had known for some while that our provisions would not stretch to another day, and here we were — no one could say where — with woods and prairie all round us, a long way from any human home, and food enough for one thin supper.", "The dogs came whining about us for their share, as they always did, and got a bare bone between them. We lay down that night knowing that if we were not hungry yet, we would be hungry by morning.", "Morning came. Plante and Roy had a good fire going and a nice pot of coffee ready, and that was breakfast, for when the bag was shaken out and turned inside out it gave up three crackers and nothing more, and the others would not hear of my leaving them; I was to put them in my pocket for my dinner. Mr. Kellogg touched me very much then. He went into his wallet and brought out a piece of tongue and a slice of fruit-cake, which he said \"he had been saving for the lady since the day before, for he saw how matters were a going.\"", "Poor man. He would have done better to take my husband's advice at the start and lay in a larger store for himself. What he had brought ran out early on the second day, and for the last two meals he had been boarding with us."],
  s41: ["The trail was still under us, and we held to it until about nine o'clock. Then the wood opened out and there was a river in front of us, broad and running fast, with a cluster of Indian wigwams standing on the far bank. The trail went straight on down into the water, which said plainly enough that the stream could be forded. We never got the chance to prove it. The break-up of the week before had stacked ice along the banks, tier upon tier, and we searched up and down for some place to bring ourselves down to the water and could not find one.", "The men shouted, and shouted again, on the chance that some straggler from the village might be within hearing with his canoe. Nothing came back to us but our own voices off the far side. What were we to do? I looked at my husband. He was still speaking cheerfully, but the care had settled into his face. \"We will follow this cross-trail down the bank of the river,\" he said. \"There must be Indians wintering near, in some of these points of wood.\"", "I will admit that our prospects dismayed me. I kept up a show of courage all the same and let nobody see how low I had sunk. There was no help in it either way — the whole party was dull and gloomy already.", "So we went on along the bank, which stood well up above the water, with a thick wood set back a little from it. Then my horse, who was mortally afraid of Indians, began to jump and prance beneath me, snorting, ears pricked, certain that an enemy stood somewhere close. I screamed up the line to my husband, who rode at the head of it, and what I felt was pure delight. \"Oh, John! John! there are Indians near - look at Jerry!\"", "At that instant a little Indian dog came out from under the bushes at the roadside and set up a barking at us. No sound was ever more welcome to me. We rode straight into the thicket, dropped down into a small hollow, and found two squaws crouched behind the brush, doing their best to keep themselves out of our sight.", "The relief came over them when Mr. Kinzie spoke to them in the Pottowattamie language. \"What are you doing here?\"", "\"Digging Indian potatoes\" - (a species of artichoke.)", "\"Where is your lodge?\"", "\"On the other side of the river.\"", "\"Good - then you have a canoe here. Can you take us across?\"", "\"Yes - the canoe is very small.\"", "They brought us down the bank to the water's edge where the canoe was lying. It was very small indeed. My husband explained to them that they would have to carry me across first and then come back for the rest of the party.", "\"Will you trust yourself alone over the river?\" asked he. \"You see that but one can cross at a time.\"", "\"Oh, yes\" - and in a moment I was laid flat in the bottom of the canoe with nothing above me but the sky. The older of the two took the paddle and knelt at my head; the younger, a girl of fourteen or fifteen, knelt down at my feet. There was exactly room for me to lie between them that way, the two of them at the opposite ends, and no room to spare.", "While all this was being got ready, Mr. Kinzie was asking the women where we had come to. They had no name for the river except \"Saumanong,\" which tells nothing, being the word they use for any large stream. But he worked out of them that the village we had passed higher up, on the other side of the water, was Wau-ban-see's, and with that he knew where we stood: on the Fox River, and probably some fifty miles from Chicago.", "To all his questions the squaw gave the same assurance, that Chicago was \"close by.\"", "\"That means,\" he said, \"that it isn't so far off as Canada. We must not be too sanguine.\"", "The men began stripping the packs off the horses, and while they worked I was paddled over. The old woman turned around and went straight back for the next load, leaving the girl behind with me for company. I found the trunk of a fallen tree and sat down on it in the snow and looked out across the dark water. I am not ashamed to own the weakness. For the first time in the whole journey, I cried. It was not hunger that brought it, nor fear, nor the cold. It was the plain desolation of spirit, the sickness of heart that comes of \"hope deferred,\" and of all the evils I know of, that one is the hardest to bear.", "The poor little squaw stood looking into my face, wondering at me and sorry for me at once. I suppose she was working out privately what a woman who rode so fine a horse and wore so comfortable a broadcloth dress could possibly find to cry about. I patted the log beside me to offer her a seat, but she would rather stand and go on watching me with that same pitying look. A young companion came out and joined her, and after a short burst of chattering in which I was plainly the subject, the two of them trotted off into the woods and left me alone with my own thoughts.", "\"What would my friends at the East think,\" I said to myself, \"if they could see me now? What would poor old Mrs. Welsh say? She who warned me that if I came away so far to the West, I should break my heart?", "Would she not rejoice to find how likely her prediction was to be fulfilled?\"", "That was enough to rouse me. I dried my face, and by the time my husband and his party and every horse and every article of luggage were over the water, I had my cheerfulness back again and was ready for whatever came next."],
  s42: ["The old squaw led and we went after her, and her lodge stood only a little way back among the trees. I had never once been inside an Indian lodge. I had looked into them — there are always a cluster of them standing around the Interpreter's house at the Portage, and I had peeped in at those — but I had never gone through a door.", "Inside, everything had its place. Four sticks of wood laid in a square in the middle of the floor served for a hearth, and the fire burned inside that square, and the smoke went up and out through the opening at the top. The mats that made the walls were new and neatly done. Bags of their own manufacture hung against the sides from the poles of the frame, holding dried food and whatever else the family counted as treasure, and from the cross-poles hung ladles and small kettles and wooden bowls. In the center, swinging from an iron chain, a large kettle hung over a thin fire, and in it something dark was seething that I did not care to look at twice. Mats were spread on the floor between the fire and the outer wall, and my husband told me to sit down there and make myself comfortable.", "When an Indian meets a white person the first thing asked for is bread. They are exceedingly fond of it. So I was not surprised, after my husband had gone out of the lodge, when the woman opened our talk with a timid \"pe-qua-zhe-gun choh-kay-go\" — I have no bread.", "I shook my head. I tried to make her understand that giving was beyond me, that I had not so much as had my own breakfast. She got my meaning at once. She took down a bowl and ladled it full of Indian potatoes out of the kettle on the fire and put it in front of me. I was in no condition to be fastidious. My appetite had a good deal to do with it, I am sure — but I thought them delicious.", "Two little girls belonged to the lodge, and they sat and stared at me with an admiration and an astonishment that only grew when I took my little Prayer book out of my pocket and began to read. They had certainly never seen a book in their lives. What amused me was their manners: they kept their faces carefully turned away from me while they asked their mother what I could possibly be doing, and listened to what she answered.", "I was reading when a sound came from the doorway — a sudden \"hogh!\" — and the mat that hung across the entrance lifted and a man came in with that springing, graceful bound that belongs only to them. The master of the lodge. He had been out after ducks and was just back. He was tall and finely made, with an open, cheerful face, and while his wife told him quietly who I was he was taking off his accoutrements, as unembarrassed and as well-bred as any gentleman I ever watched receive news of a guest.", "My husband came in not long after. He had been seeing to the horses, and helping the men get their fire going and their tent up, which the rising wind was making no easy work.", "From the Indian he had learned where we were. This was the Big Woods, or Piché's Grove, called so for a Frenchman of that name who lived not far off. The river we had crossed was the Fox River. He would guide us to Piché's, and from there the road ran perfectly plain, or he would take us the whole way into Chicago if we liked that better — but his advice was that we stay camped where we were for the day, because there was a storm coming. In the meantime he would go and shoot ducks for our dinner and our supper. We gave him powder and shot, and he went straight back out after the game.", "I had a roll of scarlet ribbon in my pocket. I had put it there when we left home, thinking a stout string might be wanted for something. I took it out now and cut two yards for each of the little girls with the knife that hung at my neck. They were delighted. Their mother took each length and divided it in half and tied a piece to each of the small clubs their hair was knotted into at the temples. Then the two of them looked at each other and laughed and cried \"Saum!\" and their mother laughed with them — though she seemed to me to be holding some of it back, not quite willing to show a stranger how proud she was of them.", "When the tent was in order my husband came to fetch me, and we said our thanks to our friends in the wigwam and went out.", "The storm had arrived. The trees around us were bending and cracking, and the whole air was full of wild-fowl going south ahead of the blast, screaming and quacking as they came. Our tent stood among the trees a short way from the water. My husband walked me down to the bank so that I could see, for one minute, what we had gotten out of. The wind came down out of the north in a perfect hurricane. The river ran loaded with masses of snow and ice, dancing along on the torrent, and above the torrent went thousands of birds, and the woods rang with the noise of them until you could hardly think.", "One hour later and the crossing would have been impossible. There would have been nothing left for us to do but stop on the far bank and starve there in the wilderness. I do not know how any of us could be grateful enough to the kind Providence that had carried us through such dangers.", "The men had felled an immense tree and built the fire up against the trunk of it. But the wind would not settle. Every five minutes it swung, and the tent filled with smoke, and I had to get out into the open just to breathe. Then I would go and sit on one end of that huge log, as close to the flames as I could manage, because the cold was dismal — and the wind seemed to me to have a will of its own and a spiteful one, since whichever side I chose was the side the smoke and the hot ashes came at, puffing straight into my face until I was half blind. My veil was no defense. Neither was a silk handkerchief. I was thankful when our huntsmen came back with a great many ducks, because plucking them and getting them ready with the men gave me something to think about besides myself.", "We borrowed a kettle from our Indian friends. It was not remarkably clean, so we warmed a little water in it and scoured it out with prairie hay before the birds went in. With that and a bowl of Indian potatoes, which our kind neighbors made us a present of, we had an excellent soup before long.", "Between the cold and the smoke and the ashes and cinders driving at me all afternoon, it was the most uncomfortable stretch of the journey so far, and I was glad when the dark came and I could crawl into the tent and pull the blankets over me and be out of reach of all three at once.", "In the night the storm came on ten times worse. Trees were going down all around the camp, and every crash brought us up out of whatever sleep we had, because there was no telling which one was coming down on us. Tired as we were, we hardly slept at all. It was the morning that showed us properly what had been happening in the dark. Within sight of the tent, fifty trees at least — giants, every one of them — lay flat on the ground.", "We ate our scanty breakfast and got mounted, and then had to pick our way out foot by foot, the road so blocked with fallen trunks that there was barely a line through them."],
  s43: ["Our guide came to us early and walked us out of the timber with a care I only understood afterward, when I saw how easily a person might have wandered in it. He kept pointing up into the branches. Bee-trees, he said; that grove was famous for them. Then he set off at a long trot and held it, and a little after nine he brought us out at Piché's — a log cabin on a rise, its face turned east over a prairie so broad it seemed to have no other edge. We had been thinking about food since first light. Some of the party knew Piché of old. The door opened on a room full of people, and not one of them was him. Travelers, a few. Indians, a great many.", "There was nothing to keep us there but the fire, a bright one burning in the clay chimney, and we stood at it long enough to feel our hands again. A man in Quaker dress came forward to answer our questions. He was bound for Chicago himself, he said, and would take us there. So we thanked our Indian friend and paid him well for all the trouble he had so kindly taken on our account, and he went back the way he had come.", "From Piché's the prairie ran unbroken to the Du Page. Between the two forks of that river, our new acquaintance — Mr. Dogherty — told us we would find the house of a Mr. Hawley, and at it a comfortable dinner. I held on to that sentence for a good many miles.", "The cold was past anything I had words for. The wind came over that open ground with nothing standing in its way, and it did not so much chill the skin as go straight through to the middle of me and settle there. When my feet went numb I beat them against the saddle to bring the blood back, and I kept beating until they were too bruised to beat any longer. Nothing offered itself. Not a house, not a wigwam, not so much as a clump of trees to put at our backs, mile after weary mile. Then the west fork of the Du Page, frozen — but not hard enough to bear the horses. There was nothing for it but to cut them a road. It took time. The ice had thickened to several inches in that one bitter night. Plante went ahead with the axe and cut as far as his arms would reach, then mounted one of the hardy little ponies and broke the ice in front of him until he had opened a passage clear to the opposite shore.", "The horses shook as they were reined in among the floating pieces, and it was a pitiful thing to watch. Those of us waiting on the bank in that wind were in no position to pity anybody. Brunet must have arrived at the same conclusion, because he did what Brunet always did and went in directly behind Plante, out of his turn, and stood quaking at his heels, turning his head now and again to look back at the rest of us, as much as to say, I've got ahead of you, this time. We were all over at last. We put the spurs to them and did not slow until Hawley's came up out of the flat ground ahead — a large, roomy house near the east fork of the river.", "The good woman of the place took us in and had us warm and comfortable in a quarter of an hour, and it was like stepping back into a settled country. She began on dinner at once. We watched her the whole time with the frank staring eyes of animals. She reached a huge ham down from the rafters and cut innumerable slices off it; she broke a dozen and more fine fresh eggs into a pan, ready for frying; she mixed a johnny-cake and propped it against a board in front of the fire to bake. With that bright fire going the business still took an unconscionable while. It was done at last, and she might well stare at the travelers' appetites we had carried in with us. She had no way of knowing what short commons we had been on for two days.", "We asked, and learned that if we pushed on we could make Lawton's, on the Aux Plaines, that same night — and that would put us within twelve miles of Chicago. We did not waste a minute we did not have to waste. We were in the saddle again as soon after dinner as it could be managed.", "The east fork was worse than the west had been. Something had already broken the ice — the current, or riders ahead of us who had cut their own way through — and it was coming down in cakes the size of doors. The horses had to make a rush of it. The water was high and running in a torrent, and if I had not been up on Jerry, the tallest horse in the cavalcade, I would have gone through it soaked to the waist.", "As it was I was well frightened, and I took hold of bridle and mane together and did not let go of either. After that we traveled as fast as the ground allowed, because the light was going and we meant to be in before dark.", "Mr. Dogherty, a tall man who sat bolt upright in the saddle, half Quaker and half Methodist, spent those miles doing his best to entertain me. He laid out a thorough schedule of his religious opinions, together with the Scripture on which each of them rested. He was a good deal of a perfectionist, and it was plain that he regarded himself with no small satisfaction as a living illustration of his favorite doctrine.", "\"St. John says,\" — this was the style of it — \"St. John says, 'He that is born of God, doth not commit sin.' Now, if I am born of God, I don't commit sin.\"", "I was too cold and too weary to take up the point, so I let him have the whole of it his own way. I believe he must have thought me rather a dull companion. He gave me the credit, at least, of being a good listener."],
  s44: ["It was nearly dark when we came up on Lawton's. The Aux Plaines was frozen over and the house stood on the other side of it. We shouted, and kept shouting, until a man came out of the building; then he set about cutting the ice and bringing a canoe across to us, and by the time he had done it we could no longer make out one object from another in the dark.", "It was a very comfortable house, Lawton's, once we were finally in it — carpeted, with a warm stove, quite in civilized style. Mr. Weeks, the man who had brought us over the water, was the major-domo there during the temporary absence of Mr. Lawton.", "Mrs. Lawton was a young woman, and not ill-looking. She complained bitterly of the loneliness of her condition, and of having been brought out there into the woods, which was a thing she hadn't expected when she came from the East. We did not ask her what expectations she had brought with her to a wild, unsettled country. We only tried to comfort her with the assurance that things would grow better in a few years. She said she didn't mean to wait for that. She should go back to her family in the East, if Mr. Lawton didn't invite some of her young friends to come and stay with her, and make it agreeable."],
  s45: ["Rising the next morning, we could hardly take it in that only twelve miles of prairie now lay between us and Chicago le Désiré, as I could not help naming it.", "We could look clear across the plain, and on its farthest verge stood two tall trees. My husband pointed them out to me: he had put them in the ground himself, as a boy. They had grown lofty enough to serve as landmarks, and they stayed in sight the whole way as we followed the beaten road. I kept saying it over to myself. There live the friends I am so longing to see. There will end all our trials and hardships.", "A Mr. Wentworth fell in with us on the road, and we asked him after the welfare of the family, from whom we had received no intelligence in a long while. When we reached Chicago he took us to a little tavern at the forks of the river. That part of the place was then called Wolf Point, from an Indian who had lived there named Moaway, or the Wolf.", "\"Dear me,\" said the old landlady at the little tavern, \"what dreadful cold weather you must have had to travel in! Why, two days ago the river was all open here, and now it's frozen hard enough for folks to cross a-horseback!\"", "For all that assurance, my husband did not like to venture the horses on it. He determined to leave them where they stood and go on foot to his mother's and sister's house, about half a mile off.", "We set out walking, first over the ice, then down the northern bank of the river. As we came near the house we were spotted by Genevieve, a half-breed servant of the family. She did not wait to salute us. She flew into the house crying, \"Oh! Madame Kinzie, who do you think has come? Monsieur John and Madame John, all the way from Fort Winnebago on foot!\"", "In another minute we were in the arms of our dear, kind friends. A messenger was sent off to the garrison for the rest of the family, and for that day at least I was the wonder and the admiration of the whole circle, for the dangers I had seen."],
  s46: ["Fort Dearborn in that day was the same collection of buildings that stands there now, in better preservation then, though even then considerably dilapidated. They had been erected in 1816 under the supervision of Captain Hezekiah Bradley, and there was a story about him that went round the post. His patriotic regard for the interests of the Government was such, people said, that he obliged the soldiers to fashion wooden pins instead of drawing spikes and nails out of stores, and to fasten the timbers of the buildings with those; and that he called on the junior officers to help in the construction alongside the men whose business it was. If it is true, the captain must have been laboring under the delusion — excusable in a man who has lived long on the frontier — that a Government thanks its servants for an excess of economical zeal.", "The fort was inclosed by high pickets, with bastions at the alternate angles. Large gates opened to the north and the south, and there were small posterns here and there for the accommodation of the people inside. The bank of the river that stretches west, covered now by the light-house buildings and inclosed by docks, was then given over to the root-houses of the garrison. South of the pickets lay the parade-ground, and beyond that the company gardens, well filled with currant-bushes and young fruit-trees.", "The fort stood at what anyone would naturally take for the mouth of the river. It was not. In those days the river came down, took a turn, and swept round the promontory the fort was built on, running south, and did not join the lake until half a mile below. The fort buildings stood on the right bank. The left was a long spit of land extending out from the northern shore, of which it formed a part. In 1833 the United States Engineers, employed to construct a harbor at this point, cut through that portion of the left bank and threw out the piers; after which the water overflowed the whole long tongue of land, and, encroaching steadily on the southern bank, robbed it of many valuable acres — while by the same action of that vast body of water an accretion was constantly taking place on the north of the harbor.", "The residence of Jean Baptiste Beaubien stood in this period between the gardens and the river-bank. Farther south still was a rickety tenement, built many years before by Mr. John Dean, the sutler of the post. A short time after the growth of Chicago began, the lake worked in under its foundations by gradual encroachment, and the building tumbled backward down the bank and lay there a long while, a melancholy spectacle.", "On the northern bank of the river, directly facing the fort, was the family mansion of my husband. It was a long, low building, a range of four or five rooms, with a piazza running the length of its front. Between it and the river lay a broad green space, inclosed, and shaded by a row of Lombardy poplars. Two immense cottonwood-trees stood in the rear of the building; one of them remains to this day, an ancient landmark. A fine, well-cultivated garden extended north from the house, and around it were grouped the various buildings belonging to the establishment — dairy, bake-house, lodging-house for the Frenchmen, and stables.", "Between the house and the lake ran a vast range of sand-hills, grown over with stunted cedars, pines, and dwarf-willow-trees. The lake itself was at this time not more than thirty rods distant.", "Proceeding from that point along the northern bank of the river, you came first to the Agency House — Cobweb Castle, as it had been denominated during the long years a bachelor lived in it, and the sobriquet adhered to it ever after. It stood at what is now the southwest corner of Wolcott and North Water Streets. Many will still remember it: a substantial, compact little building of logs hewed and squared, with a center, two wings, and, strictly speaking, two tails — because when there was found no more room for additions at the sides, they were put on in the rear, wherever a vacant spot could be found.", "These appendages did nothing to mar the symmetry of the whole as viewed from the front. But when, in the process of the town's improvement, a street was maliciously opened directly in the rear of the building, the whole establishment with its comical little adjuncts became a constant source of amusement to everybody who passed. No matter. There were pleasant, happy hours passed under that odd-shaped roof, as a good many of Chicago's early settlers can testify.", "Grouped around the Agency House was a collection of log buildings, the residences of the different people in the employ of the Government who belonged to that establishment — blacksmith, striker, and laborers. These were for the most part Canadians or half-breeds, with sometimes a stray Yankee among them, to set all things going by his activity and enterprise.", "There was still another house on the north side of the river, built by a former resident by the name of Miller, but he had removed to the Rivière du Chemin, or Trail Creek, which about this time people were beginning to call Michigan City. His house stood near the forks of the river. It was standing empty.", "On the southern bank, between the fort and the Point — the forks of the river went by that name then — there was no house at all. The land was a low wet prairie, scarcely affording good walking in the driest summer weather, and at other seasons absolutely impassable. A muddy streamlet, or, as it is called in this country, a slew, wound around from about the present site of the Tremont House and fell into the river at the foot of State Street. At the Point, on the south side, stood a house just completed by Mark Beaubien. It was a pretentious white two-story building with bright-blue wooden shutters, the admiration of all the little circle at Wolf Point. A canoe ferry was kept there to transport people across the south branch of the river.", "Facing down the river from the west there was, first, a small tavern kept by Mr. Wentworth, familiarly known as Old Geese — not from any want of shrewdness on his part, but in compliment to one of his own cant expressions. Near him stood two or three log cabins occupied by Robinson, the Pottowattamie chief, and some of his wife's connections. Billy Caldwell, the Sau-ga-nash, lived there too at times, with his wife, who was a daughter of Nee-scot-nee-meg, one of the most famous chiefs of the nation. A little remote from these residences stood a small square log building, originally designed for a school-house, but used sometimes as a place of worship whenever any itinerant minister presented himself.", "The family of Clybourn had before this time established themselves near their present residence on the North Branch; they called their place New Virginia. Four miles up the South Branch stood an old building that was at one time an object of great interest, having been the theater of some stirring events during the troubles of 1812. It was denominated Lee's Place, or Hardscrabble. A settler named Heacock was living in it at that time.", "Because of the badness of the roads the greater part of the year, the usual mode of communication between the fort and the Point was by water — a boat rowed up the river, or a canoe paddled by some skilful hand. By the same means an intercourse was kept up between the residents of the fort and the Agency House.", "There were at this time two companies of soldiers in the garrison, but the officers were thin on the ground. Lieutenant Furman had died the autumn previous. Several of the others were away on furlough. In the absence of Major Fowle and Captain Scott, the command devolved on Lieutenant Hunter. Besides him there were Lieutenants Engle and Foster, the latter unmarried. Dr. Finley, the post surgeon, was also absent, and his place was supplied by Dr. Harmon, a gentleman from Vermont.", "My husband's mother, his two sisters and his brother lived at the Agency House. The family residence near the lake was occupied by J. N. Bailey, the postmaster.", "In the Dean house lived a Mr. and Mrs. Forbes, who kept a school. Gholson Kercheval had a small trading establishment in one of the log buildings at Wolf Point, and John S. C. Hogan superintended the sutler's store in the garrison.", "There was also a Mr. See, lately come into the country, living at the Point, who sometimes held forth in the little school-house on a Sunday — less to the edification of his hearers than to the unmerciful slaughter of the King's English.", "That enumeration, I think, comprises all the white inhabitants of Chicago at a period less than half a century ago. To many who may read these pages the foregoing particulars will surely appear uninteresting. But to those who come to visit Chicago, and still more to those who come to make it their home, it may not be without interest to look back at its first beginnings, to contemplate the almost magical change a few years have wrought, and out of that past to augur the marvelous prosperity of the future."],
  s47: ["Where the name Chicago came from is still argued over. Some of the Indians derive it from the fitch, or polecat; others from the wild onion that once grew thick in these woods. But all of them agree on the one point: the place took its name from an old chief who was drowned in the stream in former times. Carefully as the tradition has been kept, that drowning must belong to a very remote period, and there is an old French manuscript, brought over from France by General Cass, that makes it evident.", "The paper purports to be a letter from M. de Ligney, at Green Bay, to M. de Siette, among the Illinois, and it is dated as early as 1726. In it the place is designated Chica-goux. The same orthography turns up in old family letters from the beginning of the present century.", "When the Indians give the early history of Chicago, they say it with great simplicity: the first white man who settled here was a negro.", "They meant Jean Baptiste Point-au-Sable, a native of St. Domingo, who about the year 1796 found his way into this remote region and began a life among the Indians. There is usually a strong affection between the two races, and Jean Baptiste imposed upon his new friends by making them believe he had been a great chief among the whites. Perhaps he was disgusted at not being elected to a similar dignity by the Pottowattamies. At any rate he left this vicinity, and ended his days at Peoria under the roof of his friend Glamorgan, another St. Domingo negro, who had got large Spanish grants in St. Louis and the country around it, and who at one time was in the enjoyment of an extensive landed estate.", "Point-au-Sable had made some improvements at Chicago. A Frenchman named Le Mai took possession of them and began trading with the Indians. After a few years Le Mai's establishment was purchased by John Kinzie, Esq., who was living at that time at Bertrand — Parc aux Vaches, as they called it then — near Niles, in Michigan. For nearly twenty years, the military excepted, this gentleman was the only white inhabitant of Northern Illinois, and some particulars of his early life may not be uninteresting."],
  s48: ["He was born at Quebec in 1163. His mother had been married before, to a gentleman by the name of Haliburton, and that marriage produced one daughter — the woman who would become the mother of General Fleming, of Nicholas Low, Esq., and of Mrs. Charles King, of New York. She is remembered as a lady of remarkable beauty and accomplishments. Of the second marriage there was one child only, and that was Mr. Kinzie. He was still an infant when his father died. His mother married a third time, a Mr. Forsyth, and the family afterward moved to the city of New York.", "At ten or eleven he was sent to school at Williamsburg, on Long Island, along with two of his half-brothers. Every Saturday a negro servant came out from the city to collect the children and carry them home until Monday morning. One Saturday the man arrived to find the school in an uproar. Johnny Kinzie was gone. They hunted in every direction, turned out every corner of the place, and it came to nothing. The boy was not there to be found.", "The news had to be carried home to his mother. Some said he had drowned. Others said he had only wandered off and would come back on his own. Weeks went by, and then months, and at last he was given up and mourned as a dead child. All that while he was carrying out a plan he had been forming for a long time: to get to Quebec, the city he had been born in, and make his own way in the world.", "However he managed it, he crossed from Williamsburg over to New York, and at one of the docks on the North River he found a sloop bound up to Albany and took passage in her. Somewhere on the river a gentleman noticed him — a small boy traveling alone — and grew curious enough to ask him what his business was.", "\"He was going to Quebec, where he had some friends.\"", "\"Had he the means to carry him there?\"", "\"Not much, but he thought he could get along.\"", "As it happened, the gentleman was bound for Quebec himself. He took the boy in charge, paid his way the entire distance, and finally left him standing in the streets of the city — a city where, whatever he had told himself, he knew nobody at all.", "He walked about for some time, putting his head into one \"store\" and workshop after another. At last he stepped into a silversmith's shop, looked at the master's face, liked what he saw written in it, and asked whether he wanted an apprentice.", "\"What, you, my little fellow! What can you do?\"", "\"Anything you can teach me.\"", "\"Well, we will make a trial and see.\"", "The trial went well enough. He lived with that kind man's family better than three years, until his parents — who, in moving to Detroit, had necessarily come back into Canada — found out where he was, and he was given back to them.", "There were five younger half-brothers, all Forsyths. In the old family Bible there is an entry set down after the family had moved to Detroit, and it reads: \"George Forsyth was lost in the woods 6th August, 1775, when Henry Hays and Mark Stirling ran away and left him. The remains of George Forsyth were found by an Indian the 2d of October, 1776, close by the Prairie Ronde.\"", "That the same unhappy mother should be made to bear that particular grief twice seems a strange cruelty — a child gone in a way that is worse than a death, because it leaves the mind free to supply every horror it can invent. This is how the little brother was lost. He was walking home from school one evening when he met the colored servant-boy riding out to the common to bring in the cows. The school-house stood close by the old fort, and everything west of it was rough open country, uncultivated, known as \"the Common.\" The child asked to be taken up for a ride. The servant said no and told him to go straight home. Two other boys, a little older, were with him, and the three of them trailed after the rider a good way, still hoping to be let up. When the dark came on, the two older boys gave it up and went back. The youngest went on. The servant did not see him again on his return, and no word of him ever came to anyone, though the whole small settlement searched and kept searching — not until the next year, when, as the record says, an Indian found his remains. Nothing about them could be identified except the auburn curls of his hair and the little boots he had worn. He cannot have lived long after he lost his way; the Prairie Ronde lay too close to the settlement for him to have missed the calls and the sounding horns of the men hunting for him, if he had still been alive to hear.", "As he grew, Mr. Kinzie's restless and adventurous turn of mind kept him out on the frontier. He went into the Indian trade young, and kept establishments at Sandusky and at Maumee. Around 1800 he pushed still farther west, to St. Joseph's, in Michigan. That same year he married Mrs. McKillip, the widow of a British officer, and in 1804 he came to Chicago to make his home. That was the year the first fort was built there, by Major John Whistler.", "Little by little he opened trading-posts farther and farther out, every one of them feeding the parent post at Chicago: at Milwaukie among the Menomonees; at Rock River among the Winnebagoes and the Pottowattamies; on the Illinois River and the Kankakee among the Pottowattamies of the Prairies; and among the Kickapoos in the country they called \"Le Large\" — that wide stretch of district afterward erected into Sangamon County.", "Every post had a superintendent over it, and its full number of engagés, its string of pack-horses, its boats and canoes. From nearly all of the stations the furs and peltries came in to Chicago on the backs of the pack-horses, and the trade goods went back out to them the same way.", "Two or three vessels a year, rarely more, came in spring and fall with the supplies and the goods for the trade, and carried away the furs already gathered, taking them to Mackinac, which was the depôt for the Southwest and the American Fur Companies. In the other seasons the furs went the same distance by boat, coasting the shore of the lake."],
  s49: ["The Canadian voyageurs — the engagés — are very nearly gone from the country now, and something ought to be set down about them here while there is anyone left who remembers what they were.", "No other class of men resembled them. Whatever it is that makes a poet, they had the equivalent of it for this work, and they came into the world already fitted for it. They were tough, they were tireless, they were clever with their hands, and they were cheerful past all reason. Set any emergency in front of them and their minds bent around it. Difficulty did not baffle them. Hardship did not put them off. And they loved easily and hard: they formed attachments of the warmest kind to their bourgeois, the master who hired them, and to the native people among whom their engagements carried them.", "Montreal was the depôt — Marrialle, in their own mouths. That was where the agents went when they had a quota to fill for one of the companies or for some independent trader, and that was where they picked over the material and made their selections.", "The wages ran from four to six hundred livres a year, in the old Quebec currency. The rations were one quart of lyed corn and two ounces of tallow a day, or, as the paper had it, \"its equivalent in whatever sort of food is to be found in the Indian country.\" That clause was doing a great deal of quiet work. Men have been known to live a whole winter on fresh fish and maple-sugar, every other supply cut off from them, and to do it cheerfully.", "There was a saying about them: \"Keep an engagé to his corn and tallow, he will serve you well - give him pork and bread, and he soon gets beyond your management.\" A bargain, to them, meant exactly what its words said and nothing else, which is how the crew of the old trader M. Berthelet came to grief. He signed them at Montreal on the terms that they should eat when their bourgeois ate, and eat what he ate. He meant it as a piece of fun. The simple Canadians took it for a rare stroke of luck and spent the first hours of the voyage tasting in advance the table they had joined. Berthelet, before they started, had stuffed his pockets with crackers. Now and then, when he was quite certain nobody had an eye on him, he slipped one out and ate it. The pipes went round, and went round again. The men grew hungry. They watched for some sign that a meal was being got ready for the bourgeois, and no such sign came, and since he was fasting they held their tongues and fasted with him.", "Then it stopped being a joke and turned serious, and they could not carry it any further. They asked, in some distress, to be let out of the bargain, and settled gladly for the ordinary rations in place of the dainties they had been promising themselves.", "At Mackinac, which was where the whole fur trade came through, a man drew a small portion of his wages in advance to buy his winter outfit — his pipes and tobacco, his needles and thread, some lengths of bright-colored ribbon, red and yellow gartering, which is quality binding. Those were the small coin he would trade to the Indians for whatever little things he wanted. If he was bound for Lake Superior, or for some post so far north that such articles were not to be had at all, he added one or two smoked deer-skins to make moccasins from.", "Fitted out that way, he entered his three years. Labor while the light lasted; and then, when evening handed him back to himself, the laughing and the joking and the singing and the long stories.", "Here and there you found a man who held to the exact letter of his agreement about what kind of work he had hired on to do. As a rule, though, an engagé stood ready for whatever his bourgeois asked of him, and was as faithful in it as any vassal ever was to his chief.", "They tell a story about M. St. Jean, who traded on the Upper Mississippi. He gave one of his Frenchmen an order: go out with the others, into the forest, and cut wood. The man refused. \"He wasn't hired,\" he said, \"to chop wood.\"", "\"Ah! for what, then, were you hired?\"", "\"To steer a boat.\"", "\"Very well, steer a boat, then, since you prefer it.\"", "It was mid-winter. They walked the objector down to the river and set him in the stern of the boat, which lay where it was, held fast in the ice.", "Two hours of the employment he had contracted for, with the thermometer below zero, and he was perfectly content to go and take his place among the choppers. He never afterward thought it wise to choose his own work.", "There is an aristocracy inside the service, and it is a comical thing to watch. The engagement generally runs three years. A first-year man is a mangeur-de-lard, a pork-eater, and an hivernant — one who has a winter in the country behind him — looks down on him with a contempt that has something sovereign in it. He will not associate with him. If the pork-eater invites him to take a friendly glass, he will manufacture some excuse and decline it. I have known the most inveterate drunkard among them to be tortured by wanting that glass and still never let himself be coaxed across the line. The custom holds.", "Once his first winter is behind him the mangeur-de-lard rises out of the freshman class, and takes his own turn at lording it over whoever comes next.", "They have another habit as well: taking the name of a bourgeois and working it into some absurdity that sounds nearly like it. Kinzie came back from one man as Quinze nez, fifteen noses, and from another as Singé, monkeyfied. Mr. Kercheval was Mons. Court-cheval, short horse. The Judge of Probate was le Juge Trop-bête, too foolish. Here is a case that shows how far it can be carried.", "Mr. Shaw, an agent of the Northwest Fur Company, had spent a great many years on the frontier, and to the voyageurs he was Monsieur Le Chat. On quitting the Indian country he married a Canadian lady, and children came — several of them. His old foreman, Louis la Liberté, arrived in Montreal to pass a winter there some years after that return, and word of the marriage had reached him, and nothing would do but that he see his old bourgeois again.", "He got his chance in the Champ de Mars, where Mr. Shaw was walking with two officers. La Liberté spotted him, ran straight over, caught him by both hands, and greeted him — \"Ah! mon cher Monsieur le Chat: comment vous portez-vous?\" (My dear Mr. Cat, how do you do?)", "\"Très-bien, Louizon.\"", "\"Et comment se porte Madame la Chatte?\" (How is the mother cat?)", "\"Bien, bien, Louizon, elle est très-bien\" (She is very well.)", "\"Et tous les petits Chatons?\" (And all the kittens?)", "That was further than Mr. Shaw could go. He said shortly that the kittens were all well, turned away with his military friends, and left poor Louizon standing there thoroughly astonished at how abruptly it had ended.", "That was the shape of their isolation — everything beyond them shut away, the garrison their only society — and inside it the family of Mr. Kinzie lived contentedly enough, with every comfort about them and nearly all the luxuries besides.", "The friendship they gave the Indians came back to them, and what it grew into was no ordinary attachment. Eight years passed from Mr. Kinzie's first coming among them. Then 1812 came, and showed exactly what that attachment was.", "The Narrative that follows records some of the chief things that happened in that year."],
};
